(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("bash",{"bash/script-basics":{"html":"\u003cp>Your team runs the same six commands after every deploy: check disk space, check the service, tail the log. Doing it by hand invites mistakes at 2 a.m. A script runs the same steps the same way every time and can be reviewed like code. Bash is already on almost every Linux server, which makes it the default tool for glue work.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>How to create and run an executable script\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Variables, quoting and why quotes matter\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Arguments, exit codes and command substitution\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Why you should check the result of every important command\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"your-first-script\">Your first script\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# check-disk.sh: print the usage of the root filesystem\nusage=$(df --output=pcent / | tail -n 1 | tr -dc '0-9')\necho &quot;Root filesystem is ${usage}% full&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-meta\">#!/usr/bin/env bash\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># check-disk.sh: print the usage of the root filesystem\u003c/span>\nusage=$(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">df\u003c/span> --output=pcent / | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -n 1 | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tr\u003c/span> -dc \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;0-9&#x27;\u003c/span>)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Root filesystem is \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${usage}\u003c/span>% full&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The first line, the \u003cstrong>shebang\u003c/strong>, tells the kernel which interpreter to use. Save it, make it executable and run it:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"chmod +x check-disk.sh\n./check-disk.sh\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">chmod\u003c/span> +x check-disk.sh\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>./check-disk.sh\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Root filesystem is 28% full\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>$(command)\u003c/code> is \u003cstrong>command substitution\u003c/strong>: it runs the command and inserts its output. The sample output above will differ on your machine.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"variables-and-quoting\">Variables and quoting\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"name=&quot;deploy user&quot;        # no spaces around =\necho &quot;Hello, $name&quot;       # double quotes expand variables\necho 'Hello, $name'       # single quotes print the text literally\necho &quot;${name}_backup&quot;     # braces mark where the name ends\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">name=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;deploy user&quot;\u003c/span>        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># no spaces around =\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Hello, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$name\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>       \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># double quotes expand variables\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Hello, $name&#x27;\u003c/span>       \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># single quotes print the text literally\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${name}\u003c/span>_backup&quot;\u003c/span>     \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># braces mark where the name ends\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-warning\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Warning\u003c/div>\u003cp>Always quote variable expansions: \u003ccode>&quot;$file&quot;\u003c/code>, not \u003ccode>$file\u003c/code>. Unquoted, a value such as \u003ccode>my file.txt\u003c/code> splits into two words, and \u003ccode>rm $file\u003c/code> would try to delete \u003ccode>my\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>file.txt\u003c/code>. Quoting prevents an entire class of bugs and accidents.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003ch2 id=\"arguments-and-exit-codes\">Arguments and exit codes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Scripts receive arguments as \u003ccode>$1\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>$2\u003c/code> and so on. \u003ccode>$#\u003c/code> is the count and \u003ccode>&quot;$@&quot;\u003c/code> is all of them, preserving spaces.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"#!/usr/bin/env bash\nservice=&quot;$1&quot;\nif [ -z &quot;$service&quot; ]; then\n  echo &quot;usage: $0 SERVICE&quot; &gt;&amp;2     # &gt;&amp;2 sends the message to stderr\n  exit 2\nfi\nsystemctl is-active --quiet &quot;$service&quot;\necho &quot;exit status was $?&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-meta\">#!/usr/bin/env bash\u003c/span>\nservice=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$1\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> [ -z \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$service\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> ]; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">then\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;usage: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$0\u003c/span> SERVICE&quot;\u003c/span> &gt;&amp;2     \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># &gt;&amp;2 sends the message to stderr\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exit\u003c/span> 2\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">fi\u003c/span>\nsystemctl is-active --quiet \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$service\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;exit status was $?&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Every command ends with an \u003cstrong>exit code\u003c/strong>: \u003ccode>0\u003c/code> means success and anything else means failure. \u003ccode>$?\u003c/code> holds the last one. Shell logic depends on it, because \u003ccode>if\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>&amp;&amp;\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>||\u003c/code> all test exit codes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"mkdir -p /srv/backups &amp;&amp; echo &quot;ready&quot;          # second command runs only if the first succeeded\nsystemctl is-active shop || echo &quot;shop is down&quot;  # second runs only if the first failed\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">mkdir\u003c/span> -p /srv/backups &amp;&amp; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ready&quot;\u003c/span>          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># second command runs only if the first succeeded\u003c/span>\nsystemctl is-active shop || \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;shop is down&quot;\u003c/span>  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># second runs only if the first failed\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Use distinct exit codes for distinct problems, such as \u003ccode>2\u003c/code> for bad usage and \u003ccode>1\u003c/code> for a failed check, so that callers like cron and CI can react.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reading-a-file-line-by-line-and-testing-values\">Reading a file line by line and testing values\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"while IFS= read -r host; do\n  if ping -c1 -W2 &quot;$host&quot; &gt;/dev/null 2&gt;&amp;1; then\n    echo &quot;$host up&quot;\n  else\n    echo &quot;$host DOWN&quot;\n  fi\ndone &lt; hosts.txt\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">while\u003c/span> IFS= \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">read\u003c/span> -r host; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">do\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> ping -c1 -W2 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$host\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> &gt;/dev/null 2&gt;&amp;1; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">then\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$host\u003c/span> up&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">else\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$host\u003c/span> DOWN&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">fi\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">done\u003c/span> &lt; hosts.txt\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>IFS= read -r\u003c/code> reads each line unchanged, without trimming spaces or interpreting backslashes. Redirecting \u003ccode>&gt;/dev/null 2&gt;&amp;1\u003c/code> discards both normal output and errors. Common tests in \u003ccode>[ ... ]\u003c/code> are \u003ccode>-f\u003c/code> (file exists), \u003ccode>-d\u003c/code> (directory exists), \u003ccode>-z\u003c/code> (string is empty), \u003ccode>-n\u003c/code> (string is not empty), \u003ccode>-eq\u003c/code> (numbers equal) and \u003ccode>==\u003c/code> (strings equal).\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-cleanup-script-that-deleted-too-much\">Incident: the cleanup script that deleted too much\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A cleanup script ran \u003ccode>rm -rf $BACKUP_DIR/*\u003c/code> nightly. After a config change \u003ccode>BACKUP_DIR\u003c/code> was never set. The shell expanded the line to \u003ccode>rm -rf /*\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>Two habits would have prevented it. First, make the shell stop on unset variables with \u003ccode>set -u\u003c/code> (covered next lesson), or fail explicitly with a default error:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"backup_dir=&quot;${BACKUP_DIR:?BACKUP_DIR must be set}&quot;   # exits with a message if unset or empty\nrm -rf -- &quot;${backup_dir:?}&quot;/*\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">backup_dir=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${BACKUP_DIR:?BACKUP_DIR must be set}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>   \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># exits with a message if unset or empty\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">rm\u003c/span> -rf -- \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${backup_dir:?}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>/*\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>${VAR:?message}\u003c/code> aborts the script when the variable is missing or empty. Second, test destructive scripts by first replacing the dangerous command with \u003ccode>echo\u003c/code>, reading what \u003cem>would\u003c/em> run, then removing the \u003ccode>echo\u003c/code>. The rule: \u003cstrong>never let a variable you did not validate decide what gets deleted.\u003c/strong>\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Spaces around \u003ccode>=\u003c/code> in assignments (\u003ccode>name = value\u003c/code> runs a command called \u003ccode>name\u003c/code>)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Unquoted variables\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring exit codes, so the script reports success after a failure\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Writing scripts that only work on your laptop because of hard-coded paths\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"your-first-script","text":"Your first script"},{"id":"variables-and-quoting","text":"Variables and quoting"},{"id":"arguments-and-exit-codes","text":"Arguments and exit codes"},{"id":"reading-a-file-line-by-line-and-testing-values","text":"Reading a file line by line and testing values"},{"id":"incident-the-cleanup-script-that-deleted-too-much","text":"Incident: the cleanup script that deleted too much"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"GNU Bash reference manual","u":"https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/bash.html"},{"t":"POSIX shell command language","u":"https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799/utilities/V3_chap02.html"},{"t":"ShellCheck","u":"https://www.shellcheck.net/"}],"text":"Your team runs the same six commands after every deploy: check disk space, check the service, tail the log. Doing it by hand invites mistakes at 2 a.m. A script runs the same steps the same way every time and can be reviewed like code. Bash is already on almost every Linux server, which makes it the default tool for glue work. What you will learn How to create and run an executable script Variables, quoting and why quotes matter Arguments, exit codes and command substitution Why you should check the result of every important command Your first script #!/usr/bin/env bash # check-disk.sh: print the usage of the root filesystem usage=$( df --output=pcent / | tail -n 1 | tr -dc &#x27;0-9&#x27; ) echo \"Root filesystem is ${usage} % full\" The first line, the shebang , tells the kernel which interpreter to use. Save it, make it executable and run it: $ chmod +x check-disk.sh $ ./check-disk.sh Root filesystem is 28% full $(command) is command substitution : it runs the command and inserts its output. The sample output above will differ on your machine. Variables and quoting name= \"deploy user\" # no spaces around = echo \"Hello, $name \" # double quotes expand variables echo &#x27;Hello, $name&#x27; # single quotes print the text literally echo \" ${name} _backup\" # braces mark where the name ends Warning Always quote variable expansions: \"$file\" , not $file . Unquoted, a value such as my file.txt splits into two words, and rm $file would try to delete my and file.txt . Quoting prevents an entire class of bugs and accidents. Arguments and exit codes Scripts receive arguments as $1 , $2 and so on. $# is the count and \"$@\" is all of them, preserving spaces. #!/usr/bin/env bash service= \" $1 \" if [ -z \" $service \" ]; then echo \"usage: $0 SERVICE\" >&2 # >&2 sends the message to stderr exit 2 fi systemctl is-active --quiet \" $service \" echo \"exit status was $?\" Every command ends with an exit code : 0 means success and anything else means failure. $? holds the last one. Shell logic depends on it, because if , && and || all test exit codes. mkdir -p /srv/backups && echo \"ready\" # second command runs only if the first succeeded systemctl is-active shop || echo \"shop is down\" # second runs only if the first failed Use distinct exit codes for distinct problems, such as 2 for bad usage and 1 for a failed check, so that callers like cron and CI can react. Reading a file line by line and testing values while IFS= read -r host; do if ping -c1 -W2 \" $host \" >/dev/null 2>&1; then echo \" $host up\" else echo \" $host DOWN\" fi done \u003c hosts.txt IFS= read -r reads each line unchanged, without trimming spaces or interpreting backslashes. Redirecting >/dev/null 2>&1 discards both normal output and errors. Common tests in [ ... ] are -f (file exists), -d (directory exists), -z (string is empty), -n (string is not empty), -eq (numbers equal) and == (strings equal). Incident: the cleanup script that deleted too much Incident A cleanup script ran rm -rf $BACKUP_DIR/* nightly. After a config change BACKUP_DIR was never set. The shell expanded the line to rm -rf /* . Two habits would have prevented it. First, make the shell stop on unset variables with set -u (covered next lesson), or fail explicitly with a default error: backup_dir= \" ${BACKUP_DIR:?BACKUP_DIR must be set} \" # exits with a message if unset or empty rm -rf -- \" ${backup_dir:?} \" /* ${VAR:?message} aborts the script when the variable is missing or empty. Second, test destructive scripts by first replacing the dangerous command with echo , reading what would run, then removing the echo . The rule: never let a variable you did not validate decide what gets deleted. Common mistakes Spaces around = in assignments ( name = value runs a command called name ) Unquoted variables Ignoring exit codes, so the script reports success after a failure Writing scripts that only work on your laptop because of hard-coded paths"},"bash/control-flow-and-functions":{"html":"\u003cp>A health check that only prints &quot;OK&quot; is not much use. You need scripts that make decisions: retry a flaky call, branch on which environment you are in, and reuse the same logic for ten servers. Control flow and functions give your scripts that structure.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>if\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>case\u003c/code> and the difference between \u003ccode>[ ]\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>[[ ]]\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>for\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>while\u003c/code> loops\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Functions, local variables and return codes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A retry helper you can reuse\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"conditionals\">Conditionals\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"if [[ &quot;$ENVIRONMENT&quot; == &quot;prod&quot; &amp;&amp; -f /etc/shop/maintenance ]]; then\n  echo &quot;Maintenance flag is set in production&quot;\nelif [[ -z &quot;$ENVIRONMENT&quot; ]]; then\n  echo &quot;ENVIRONMENT is not set&quot; &gt;&amp;2\nelse\n  echo &quot;Continuing in $ENVIRONMENT&quot;\nfi\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> [[ \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$ENVIRONMENT\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> == \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;prod&quot;\u003c/span> &amp;&amp; -f /etc/shop/maintenance ]]; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">then\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Maintenance flag is set in production&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">elif\u003c/span> [[ -z \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$ENVIRONMENT\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> ]]; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">then\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ENVIRONMENT is not set&quot;\u003c/span> &gt;&amp;2\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">else\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Continuing in \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$ENVIRONMENT\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">fi\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>[[ ... ]]\u003c/code> is Bash&#39;s extended test. It supports \u003ccode>&amp;&amp;\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>||\u003c/code>, pattern matching (\u003ccode>[[ $name == web-* ]]\u003c/code>) and does not word-split unquoted variables, so it is safer than the older \u003ccode>[ ... ]\u003c/code>. Use \u003ccode>(( ... ))\u003c/code> for arithmetic: \u003ccode>if (( count &gt; 3 )); then\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>case\u003c/code> is cleaner than a chain of \u003ccode>elif\u003c/code> when you compare one value with several patterns:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"case &quot;$1&quot; in\n  start)   systemctl start shop ;;\n  stop)    systemctl stop shop ;;\n  restart) systemctl restart shop ;;\n  *)       echo &quot;usage: $0 {start|stop|restart}&quot; &gt;&amp;2; exit 2 ;;\nesac\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">case\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$1\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span>\n  start)   systemctl start shop ;;\n  stop)    systemctl stop shop ;;\n  restart) systemctl restart shop ;;\n  *)       \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;usage: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$0\u003c/span> {start|stop|restart}&quot;\u003c/span> &gt;&amp;2; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exit\u003c/span> 2 ;;\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">esac\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"loops\">Loops\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"for host in web01 web02 web03; do\n  echo &quot;== $host ==&quot;\n  ssh &quot;$host&quot; uptime\ndone\n\nfor f in /var/log/app/*.log; do\n  [[ -e &quot;$f&quot; ]] || continue      # skip when the glob matches nothing\n  gzip -- &quot;$f&quot;\ndone\n\nattempt=1\nwhile (( attempt &lt;= 5 )); do\n  curl -fsS http://localhost:8080/health &amp;&amp; break\n  sleep $(( attempt * 2 ))\n  (( attempt++ ))\ndone\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> host \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> web01 web02 web03; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">do\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;== \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$host\u003c/span> ==&quot;\u003c/span>\n  ssh \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$host\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">uptime\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">done\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> f \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> /var/log/app/*.\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">log\u003c/span>; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">do\u003c/span>\n  [[ -e \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$f\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> ]] || \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">continue\u003c/span>      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># skip when the glob matches nothing\u003c/span>\n  gzip -- \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$f\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">done\u003c/span>\n\nattempt=1\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">while\u003c/span> (( attempt &lt;= \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5\u003c/span> )); \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">do\u003c/span>\n  curl -fsS http://localhost:8080/health &amp;&amp; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">break\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sleep\u003c/span> $(( attempt * \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span> ))\n  (( attempt++ ))\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">done\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>curl -fsS\u003c/code> fails on HTTP errors (\u003ccode>-f\u003c/code>), stays quiet (\u003ccode>-s\u003c/code>) but still shows errors (\u003ccode>-S\u003c/code>). That makes its exit code meaningful for scripts. In the last loop the wait grows each time, which is a simple \u003cstrong>backoff\u003c/strong> that gives a starting service time to become ready.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"functions\">Functions\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"log() {\n  printf '%s %s\\n' &quot;$(date '+%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S')&quot; &quot;$*&quot; &gt;&amp;2\n}\n\ncheck_port() {\n  local host=&quot;$1&quot; port=&quot;$2&quot;          # local keeps variables inside the function\n  timeout 3 bash -c &quot;cat &lt; /dev/null &gt; /dev/tcp/${host}/${port}&quot; 2&gt;/dev/null\n}\n\nif check_port db01 5432; then\n  log &quot;database reachable&quot;\nelse\n  log &quot;database unreachable&quot;\n  exit 1\nfi\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-function\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-title\">log\u003c/span>\u003c/span>() {\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">printf\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;%s %s\\n&#x27;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">$(date &#x27;+%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S&#x27;)\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;$*&quot;\u003c/span> &gt;&amp;2\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-function\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-title\">check_port\u003c/span>\u003c/span>() {\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">local\u003c/span> host=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$1\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> port=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$2\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># local keeps variables inside the function\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">timeout\u003c/span> 3 bash -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;cat &lt; /dev/null &gt; /dev/tcp/\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${host}\u003c/span>/\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${port}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> 2&gt;/dev/null\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> check_port db01 5432; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">then\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">log\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;database reachable&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">else\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">log\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;database unreachable&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exit\u003c/span> 1\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">fi\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Functions \u003cstrong>return an exit code\u003c/strong>, not text. \u003ccode>return 0\u003c/code> is success. Use \u003ccode>local\u003c/code> for every variable inside a function, otherwise it leaks into the rest of the script. Send log messages to stderr (\u003ccode>&gt;&amp;2\u003c/code>) so that stdout stays clean for data that another command might consume.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-reusable-retry-function\">A reusable retry function\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"retry() {\n  local tries=&quot;$1&quot; delay=&quot;$2&quot;; shift 2\n  local n=1\n  until &quot;$@&quot;; do\n    if (( n &gt;= tries )); then\n      log &quot;giving up after $n attempts: $*&quot;\n      return 1\n    fi\n    log &quot;attempt $n failed, retrying in ${delay}s&quot;\n    sleep &quot;$delay&quot;\n    (( n++ ))\n  done\n}\n\nretry 5 3 curl -fsS http://localhost:8080/health\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-function\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-title\">retry\u003c/span>\u003c/span>() {\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">local\u003c/span> tries=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$1\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> delay=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$2\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">shift\u003c/span> 2\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">local\u003c/span> n=1\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">until\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$@\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">do\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> (( n &gt;= tries )); \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">then\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">log\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;giving up after \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$n\u003c/span> attempts: $*&quot;\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">return\u003c/span> 1\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">fi\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">log\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;attempt \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$n\u003c/span> failed, retrying in \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${delay}\u003c/span>s&quot;\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sleep\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$delay\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\n    (( n++ ))\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">done\u003c/span>\n}\n\nretry 5 3 curl -fsS http://localhost:8080/health\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>shift 2\u003c/code> drops the first two arguments so \u003ccode>&quot;$@&quot;\u003c/code> contains only the command to run. Because the function takes any command, you can reuse it for downloads, database checks or deploy steps.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-deploy-that-succeeded-against-a-dead-app\">Incident: the deploy that &quot;succeeded&quot; against a dead app\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A deploy script restarted the service and printed &quot;Deploy complete&quot; before the application finished loading. Smoke tests then failed intermittently, and one run marked the release healthy while the service was still down.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>The script waited a fixed \u003ccode>sleep 5\u003c/code>. Startup time varies with load, so the wait was sometimes too short. Replace sleeps with a \u003cstrong>readiness loop\u003c/strong> that checks the thing you care about and fails clearly:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"systemctl restart shop\nif ! retry 20 3 curl -fsS http://localhost:8080/health &gt;/dev/null; then\n  log &quot;shop did not become healthy&quot;\n  journalctl -u shop -n 20 --no-pager &gt;&amp;2\n  exit 1\nfi\nlog &quot;shop is healthy&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">systemctl restart shop\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> ! retry 20 3 curl -fsS http://localhost:8080/health &gt;/dev/null; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">then\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">log\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;shop did not become healthy&quot;\u003c/span>\n  journalctl -u shop -n 20 --no-pager &gt;&amp;2\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exit\u003c/span> 1\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">fi\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">log\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;shop is healthy&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Now the script only reports success when the health endpoint answers, and on failure it prints the last log lines so the person on call sees the cause immediately.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"predict-the-output\">Predict the output\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> What does this script print?\u003c/p>\u003cpre class=\"predict-code\" tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>name=&quot;web 01&quot;\nfor w in $name; do echo &quot;[$w]&quot;; done\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> [web] on one line and [01] on the next.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Without quotes around $name the shell splits on spaces. Write for w in &quot;$name&quot; to keep one item.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> What is the exit status shown by echo $? at the end?\u003c/p>\u003cpre class=\"predict-code\" tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>grep -q root /etc/passwd\nfalse\necho $?\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> 1\u003c/p>\u003cp>$? holds the status of the most recent command, which is false. The grep result was overwritten.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using \u003ccode>[ $var = x ]\u003c/code> with an unquoted variable that can be empty\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting \u003ccode>local\u003c/code> in functions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Looping over \u003ccode>ls\u003c/code> output instead of a glob such as \u003ccode>*.log\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using fixed \u003ccode>sleep\u003c/code> calls instead of checking readiness\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"conditionals","text":"Conditionals"},{"id":"loops","text":"Loops"},{"id":"functions","text":"Functions"},{"id":"a-reusable-retry-function","text":"A reusable retry function"},{"id":"incident-the-deploy-that-succeeded-against-a-dead-app","text":"Incident: the deploy that succeeded against a dead app"},{"id":"predict-the-output","text":"Predict the output"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"GNU Bash reference manual","u":"https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/bash.html"},{"t":"POSIX shell command language","u":"https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799/utilities/V3_chap02.html"},{"t":"ShellCheck","u":"https://www.shellcheck.net/"}],"text":"A health check that only prints \"OK\" is not much use. You need scripts that make decisions: retry a flaky call, branch on which environment you are in, and reuse the same logic for ten servers. Control flow and functions give your scripts that structure. What you will learn if , case and the difference between [ ] and [[ ]] for and while loops Functions, local variables and return codes A retry helper you can reuse Conditionals if [[ \" $ENVIRONMENT \" == \"prod\" && -f /etc/shop/maintenance ]]; then echo \"Maintenance flag is set in production\" elif [[ -z \" $ENVIRONMENT \" ]]; then echo \"ENVIRONMENT is not set\" >&2 else echo \"Continuing in $ENVIRONMENT \" fi [[ ... ]] is Bash&#39;s extended test. It supports && , || , pattern matching ( [[ $name == web-* ]] ) and does not word-split unquoted variables, so it is safer than the older [ ... ] . Use (( ... )) for arithmetic: if (( count > 3 )); then . case is cleaner than a chain of elif when you compare one value with several patterns: case \" $1 \" in start) systemctl start shop ;; stop) systemctl stop shop ;; restart) systemctl restart shop ;; *) echo \"usage: $0 {start|stop|restart}\" >&2; exit 2 ;; esac Loops for host in web01 web02 web03; do echo \"== $host ==\" ssh \" $host \" uptime done for f in /var/log/app/*. log ; do [[ -e \" $f \" ]] || continue # skip when the glob matches nothing gzip -- \" $f \" done attempt=1 while (( attempt \u003c= 5 )); do curl -fsS http://localhost:8080/health && break sleep $(( attempt * 2 )) (( attempt++ )) done curl -fsS fails on HTTP errors ( -f ), stays quiet ( -s ) but still shows errors ( -S ). That makes its exit code meaningful for scripts. In the last loop the wait grows each time, which is a simple backoff that gives a starting service time to become ready. Functions log () { printf &#x27;%s %s\\n&#x27; \" $(date &#x27;+%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S&#x27;) \" \"$*\" >&2 } check_port () { local host= \" $1 \" port= \" $2 \" # local keeps variables inside the function timeout 3 bash -c \"cat \u003c /dev/null > /dev/tcp/ ${host} / ${port} \" 2>/dev/null } if check_port db01 5432; then log \"database reachable\" else log \"database unreachable\" exit 1 fi Functions return an exit code , not text. return 0 is success. Use local for every variable inside a function, otherwise it leaks into the rest of the script. Send log messages to stderr ( >&2 ) so that stdout stays clean for data that another command might consume. A reusable retry function retry () { local tries= \" $1 \" delay= \" $2 \" ; shift 2 local n=1 until \" $@ \" ; do if (( n >= tries )); then log \"giving up after $n attempts: $*\" return 1 fi log \"attempt $n failed, retrying in ${delay} s\" sleep \" $delay \" (( n++ )) done } retry 5 3 curl -fsS http://localhost:8080/health shift 2 drops the first two arguments so \"$@\" contains only the command to run. Because the function takes any command, you can reuse it for downloads, database checks or deploy steps. Incident: the deploy that \"succeeded\" against a dead app Incident A deploy script restarted the service and printed \"Deploy complete\" before the application finished loading. Smoke tests then failed intermittently, and one run marked the release healthy while the service was still down. The script waited a fixed sleep 5 . Startup time varies with load, so the wait was sometimes too short. Replace sleeps with a readiness loop that checks the thing you care about and fails clearly: systemctl restart shop if ! retry 20 3 curl -fsS http://localhost:8080/health >/dev/null; then log \"shop did not become healthy\" journalctl -u shop -n 20 --no-pager >&2 exit 1 fi log \"shop is healthy\" Now the script only reports success when the health endpoint answers, and on failure it prints the last log lines so the person on call sees the cause immediately. Predict the output Predict first. What does this script print? name=\"web 01\" for w in $name; do echo \"[$w]\"; done Your guess Answer: [web] on one line and [01] on the next. Without quotes around $name the shell splits on spaces. Write for w in \"$name\" t"},"bash/text-processing":{"html":"\u003cp>A 2 GB access log contains the answer to &quot;which clients hit us hardest at 14:03?&quot; You do not need a dashboard or a script file. A pipeline of small tools can answer it in seconds. This is one of the highest-value skills for operations work, because most data on a server is text.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>How pipes connect small tools\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>grep\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>cut\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>sort\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>uniq\u003c/code> for counting and filtering\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003ccode>sed\u003c/code> for substitutions and \u003ccode>awk\u003c/code> for columns and sums\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to build a pipeline step by step\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-pipeline-idea\">The pipeline idea\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Each Unix tool does one job and reads stdin and writes stdout. The pipe \u003ccode>|\u003c/code> sends one program&#39;s output to the next program&#39;s input.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"cat access.log | head -2\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cat\u003c/span> access.log | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">203.0.113.7 - - [24/Oct/2026:14:03:11 +0000] &quot;GET /cart HTTP/1.1&quot; 502 157 &quot;-&quot; &quot;Mozilla/5.0&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">198.51.100.4 - - [24/Oct/2026:14:03:12 +0000] &quot;GET /health HTTP/1.1&quot; 200 2 &quot;-&quot; &quot;kube-probe/1.29&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>This is sample data in the common web server log format. Fields are separated by spaces, so field 1 is the client address, \u003ccode>$7\u003c/code> is the path and \u003ccode>$9\u003c/code> is the status code.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"filter-and-count\">Filter and count\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"grep -c ' 502 ' access.log\ngrep -v 'kube-probe' access.log | wc -l\nawk '{print $1}' access.log | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head -3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27; 502 &#x27;\u003c/span> access.log\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">41\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -v \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;kube-probe&#x27;\u003c/span> access.log | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">18420\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>awk \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{print $1}&#x27;\u003c/span> access.log | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sort\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">uniq\u003c/span> -c | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sort\u003c/span> -rn | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">   9402 203.0.113.7\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">   5120 198.51.100.22\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    977 192.0.2.88\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The last line is a classic: extract the client address, \u003ccode>sort\u003c/code> so identical values are adjacent, \u003ccode>uniq -c\u003c/code> counts runs, and \u003ccode>sort -rn\u003c/code> puts the largest first. \u003ccode>uniq\u003c/code> only collapses \u003cem>adjacent\u003c/em> duplicates, which is why the first \u003ccode>sort\u003c/code> matters.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Tool\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Use it for\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Example\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>grep\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Keep or drop lines\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>`grep -E &#39;ERROR\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>cut\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Take fixed delimiters\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>cut -d: -f1 /etc/passwd\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>sort\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>uniq\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Order and count\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>sort -u\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>uniq -c\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>sed\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Edit text in a stream\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>sed &#39;s/old/new/g&#39;\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>awk\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Columns, conditions, math\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>awk &#39;$9==502 {n++} END {print n}&#39;\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>tr\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>wc\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>head\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>tail\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Small helpers\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>wc -l\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>tail -n 100\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"sed-substitute-and-delete\">sed: substitute and delete\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"sed 's/DEBUG/INFO/g' app.log               # replace in the output only\nsed -i.bak 's/^port=.*/port=8081/' app.conf # edit in place, keeping app.conf.bak\nsed -n '10,20p' app.log                    # print lines 10 to 20\nsed '/^#/d;/^$/d' app.conf                 # drop comments and blank lines\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">sed \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;s/DEBUG/INFO/g&#x27;\u003c/span> app.log               \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># replace in the output only\u003c/span>\nsed -i.bak \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;s/^port=.*/port=8081/&#x27;\u003c/span> app.conf \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># edit in place, keeping app.conf.bak\u003c/span>\nsed -n \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;10,20p&#x27;\u003c/span> app.log                    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># print lines 10 to 20\u003c/span>\nsed \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;/^#/d;/^$/d&#x27;\u003c/span> app.conf                 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># drop comments and blank lines\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>-i.bak\u003c/code> edits the file in place and keeps a backup, which makes risky edits reversible. Without \u003ccode>-i\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>sed\u003c/code> prints the result and leaves the file alone, so test that way first.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"awk-columns-conditions-and-sums\">awk: columns, conditions and sums\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"awk '$9 &gt;= 500 {print $9}' access.log | sort | uniq -c\nawk '{bytes += $10} END {printf &quot;%.1f MB\\n&quot;, bytes/1024/1024}' access.log\nawk -F: '$3 &gt;= 1000 {print $1}' /etc/passwd\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>awk \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;$9 &gt;= 500 {print $9}&#x27;\u003c/span> access.log | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sort\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">uniq\u003c/span> -c\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">     41 502\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">      3 503\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>awk \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{bytes += $10} END {printf &quot;%.1f MB\\n&quot;, bytes/1024/1024}&#x27;\u003c/span> access.log\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">38.4 MB\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>awk -F: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;$3 &gt;= 1000 {print $1}&#x27;\u003c/span> /etc/passwd\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">deploy\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>awk\u003c/code> runs a rule for each line: \u003ccode>pattern {action}\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>END\u003c/code> runs after the last line, which is how you print totals. \u003ccode>-F:\u003c/code> changes the field separator to a colon.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"build-pipelines-step-by-step\">Build pipelines step by step\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Never write a long pipeline in one go. Run the first stage, look at the output, then add the next stage.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"grep ' 502 ' access.log                          # 1. are these the lines I want?\ngrep ' 502 ' access.log | awk '{print $4}'       # 2. is field 4 the timestamp?\ngrep ' 502 ' access.log | awk '{print $4}' | cut -c2-18 | sort | uniq -c   # 3. per minute\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">grep \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27; 502 &#x27;\u003c/span> access.log                          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># 1. are these the lines I want?\u003c/span>\ngrep \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27; 502 &#x27;\u003c/span> access.log | awk \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{print $4}&#x27;\u003c/span>       \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># 2. is field 4 the timestamp?\u003c/span>\ngrep \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27; 502 &#x27;\u003c/span> access.log | awk \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{print $4}&#x27;\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cut\u003c/span> -c2-18 | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sort\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">uniq\u003c/span> -c   \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># 3. per minute\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-which-endpoint-is-failing\">Incident: which endpoint is failing?\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>The 502 count jumped. You need to know whether one path or the whole site is failing.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"awk '$9 == 502 {print $7}' access.log | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head -3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>awk \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;$9 == 502 {print $7}&#x27;\u003c/span> access.log | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sort\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">uniq\u003c/span> -c | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sort\u003c/span> -rn | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">     38 /cart\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">      3 /checkout\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Almost all failures hit \u003ccode>/cart\u003c/code>, so the problem is likely in the service behind that path rather than in nginx or the network. Next, check whether the failures cluster in time:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"awk '$9 == 502 {print substr($4, 2, 17)}' access.log | uniq -c\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>awk \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;$9 == 502 {print substr($4, 2, 17)}&#x27;\u003c/span> access.log | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">uniq\u003c/span> -c\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">     41 24/Oct/2026:14:03\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>All 41 happened in one minute, which points to a short event such as a restart, not a steady fault. In two commands you narrowed &quot;502s&quot; to &quot;the cart service, one minute, 14:03&quot;, which tells you where to read the next log.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using \u003ccode>uniq -c\u003c/code> without sorting first\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Editing a file with \u003ccode>sed -i\u003c/code> without a backup\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Parsing structured data such as JSON with \u003ccode>grep\u003c/code>; use \u003ccode>jq\u003c/code> for that\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting that field numbers depend on the log format\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-pipeline-idea","text":"The pipeline idea"},{"id":"filter-and-count","text":"Filter and count"},{"id":"sed-substitute-and-delete","text":"sed: substitute and delete"},{"id":"awk-columns-conditions-and-sums","text":"awk: columns, conditions and sums"},{"id":"build-pipelines-step-by-step","text":"Build pipelines step by step"},{"id":"incident-which-endpoint-is-failing","text":"Incident: which endpoint is failing?"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"GNU grep manual","u":"https://www.gnu.org/software/grep/manual/grep.html"},{"t":"GNU sed manual","u":"https://www.gnu.org/software/sed/manual/sed.html"},{"t":"GNU Awk user guide","u":"https://www.gnu.org/software/gawk/manual/"}],"text":"A 2 GB access log contains the answer to \"which clients hit us hardest at 14:03?\" You do not need a dashboard or a script file. A pipeline of small tools can answer it in seconds. This is one of the highest-value skills for operations work, because most data on a server is text. What you will learn How pipes connect small tools grep , cut , sort , uniq for counting and filtering sed for substitutions and awk for columns and sums How to build a pipeline step by step The pipeline idea Each Unix tool does one job and reads stdin and writes stdout. The pipe | sends one program&#39;s output to the next program&#39;s input. $ cat access.log | head -2 203.0.113.7 - - [24/Oct/2026:14:03:11 +0000] \"GET /cart HTTP/1.1\" 502 157 \"-\" \"Mozilla/5.0\" 198.51.100.4 - - [24/Oct/2026:14:03:12 +0000] \"GET /health HTTP/1.1\" 200 2 \"-\" \"kube-probe/1.29\" This is sample data in the common web server log format. Fields are separated by spaces, so field 1 is the client address, $7 is the path and $9 is the status code. Filter and count $ grep -c &#x27; 502 &#x27; access.log 41 $ grep -v &#x27;kube-probe&#x27; access.log | wc -l 18420 $ awk &#x27;{print $1}&#x27; access.log | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head -3 9402 203.0.113.7 5120 198.51.100.22 977 192.0.2.88 The last line is a classic: extract the client address, sort so identical values are adjacent, uniq -c counts runs, and sort -rn puts the largest first. uniq only collapses adjacent duplicates, which is why the first sort matters. Tool Use it for Example grep Keep or drop lines `grep -E &#39;ERROR cut Take fixed delimiters cut -d: -f1 /etc/passwd sort , uniq Order and count sort -u , uniq -c sed Edit text in a stream sed &#39;s/old/new/g&#39; awk Columns, conditions, math awk &#39;$9==502 {n++} END {print n}&#39; tr , wc , head , tail Small helpers wc -l , tail -n 100 sed: substitute and delete sed &#x27;s/DEBUG/INFO/g&#x27; app.log # replace in the output only sed -i.bak &#x27;s/^port=.*/port=8081/&#x27; app.conf # edit in place, keeping app.conf.bak sed -n &#x27;10,20p&#x27; app.log # print lines 10 to 20 sed &#x27;/^#/d;/^$/d&#x27; app.conf # drop comments and blank lines -i.bak edits the file in place and keeps a backup, which makes risky edits reversible. Without -i , sed prints the result and leaves the file alone, so test that way first. awk: columns, conditions and sums $ awk &#x27;$9 >= 500 {print $9}&#x27; access.log | sort | uniq -c 41 502 3 503 $ awk &#x27;{bytes += $10} END {printf \"%.1f MB\\n\", bytes/1024/1024}&#x27; access.log 38.4 MB $ awk -F: &#x27;$3 >= 1000 {print $1}&#x27; /etc/passwd deploy awk runs a rule for each line: pattern {action} . END runs after the last line, which is how you print totals. -F: changes the field separator to a colon. Build pipelines step by step Never write a long pipeline in one go. Run the first stage, look at the output, then add the next stage. grep &#x27; 502 &#x27; access.log # 1. are these the lines I want? grep &#x27; 502 &#x27; access.log | awk &#x27;{print $4}&#x27; # 2. is field 4 the timestamp? grep &#x27; 502 &#x27; access.log | awk &#x27;{print $4}&#x27; | cut -c2-18 | sort | uniq -c # 3. per minute Incident: which endpoint is failing? Incident The 502 count jumped. You need to know whether one path or the whole site is failing. $ awk &#x27;$9 == 502 {print $7}&#x27; access.log | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head -3 38 /cart 3 /checkout Almost all failures hit /cart , so the problem is likely in the service behind that path rather than in nginx or the network. Next, check whether the failures cluster in time: $ awk &#x27;$9 == 502 {print substr($4, 2, 17)}&#x27; access.log | uniq -c 41 24/Oct/2026:14:03 All 41 happened in one minute, which points to a short event such as a restart, not a steady fault. In two commands you narrowed \"502s\" to \"the cart service, one minute, 14:03\", which tells you where to read the next log. Common mistakes Using uniq -c without sorting first Editing a file with sed -i without a backup Parsing structured data s"},"bash/advanced-patterns":{"html":"\u003cp>Once a script is more than a dozen lines, you need real argument handling, data structures and ways to generate files. Bash has all of these, with a few sharp edges you should know about.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Parsing flags with \u003ccode>getopts\u003c/code> and writing a usage message\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Indexed and associative arrays, and quoting them correctly\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Here-documents for config files and messages\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Process substitution, \u003ccode>mapfile\u003c/code> and parameter expansion\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"options-with-getopts\">Options with getopts\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"#!/usr/bin/env bash\nset -euo pipefail\n\nusage() { echo &quot;usage: $0 [-e env] [-n] [-v] service...&quot; &gt;&amp;2; exit 2; }\n\nenv=&quot;staging&quot;; dry_run=0; verbose=0\nwhile getopts &quot;:e:nvh&quot; opt; do\n  case &quot;$opt&quot; in\n    e) env=&quot;$OPTARG&quot; ;;\n    n) dry_run=1 ;;\n    v) verbose=1 ;;\n    h) usage ;;\n    :) echo &quot;option -$OPTARG needs a value&quot; &gt;&amp;2; usage ;;\n    \\?) echo &quot;unknown option -$OPTARG&quot; &gt;&amp;2; usage ;;\n  esac\ndone\nshift $((OPTIND - 1))\n[ &quot;$#&quot; -ge 1 ] || usage\n\nfor svc in &quot;$@&quot;; do\n  echo &quot;deploying $svc to $env (dry-run=$dry_run)&quot;\ndone\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-meta\">#!/usr/bin/env bash\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span> -euo pipefail\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-function\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-title\">usage\u003c/span>\u003c/span>() { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;usage: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$0\u003c/span> [-e env] [-n] [-v] service...&quot;\u003c/span> &gt;&amp;2; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exit\u003c/span> 2; }\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">env\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;staging&quot;\u003c/span>; dry_run=0; verbose=0\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">while\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">getopts\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;:e:nvh&quot;\u003c/span> opt; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">do\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">case\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$opt\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span>\n    e) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">env\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$OPTARG\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> ;;\n    n) dry_run=1 ;;\n    v) verbose=1 ;;\n    h) usage ;;\n    :) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;option -\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$OPTARG\u003c/span> needs a value&quot;\u003c/span> &gt;&amp;2; usage ;;\n    \\?) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;unknown option -\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$OPTARG\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> &gt;&amp;2; usage ;;\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">esac\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">done\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">shift\u003c/span> $((OPTIND - \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>))\n[ \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$#\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> -ge 1 ] || usage\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> svc \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$@\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">do\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;deploying \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$svc\u003c/span> to \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$env\u003c/span> (dry-run=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$dry_run\u003c/span>)&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">done\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A trailing colon after a letter in the option string means the option takes a value, and a leading colon turns on silent error handling so you print your own messages. After the loop, \u003ccode>shift\u003c/code> removes the parsed options so \u003ccode>&quot;$@&quot;\u003c/code> holds only the remaining arguments.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"arrays\">Arrays\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"hosts=(web-1 web-2 &quot;db 1&quot;)\nhosts+=(cache-1)\necho &quot;${#hosts[@]} hosts, first is ${hosts[0]}&quot;\nfor h in &quot;${hosts[@]}&quot;; do\n  echo &quot;checking: $h&quot;\ndone\n\ndeclare -A port=([web]=8080 [api]=9000)\nport[metrics]=9100\nfor name in &quot;${!port[@]}&quot;; do\n  echo &quot;$name uses port ${port[$name]}&quot;\ndone\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">hosts=(web-1 web-2 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;db 1&quot;\u003c/span>)\nhosts+=(cache-1)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${#hosts[@]}\u003c/span> hosts, first is \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${hosts[0]}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> h \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${hosts[@]}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">do\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;checking: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$h\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">done\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">declare\u003c/span> -A port=([web]=8080 [api]=9000)\nport[metrics]=9100\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> name \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${!port[@]}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">do\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$name\u003c/span> uses port \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${port[$name]}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">done\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The quoting is the whole point: \u003ccode>&quot;${hosts[@]}&quot;\u003c/code> expands to one word per element and preserves the element with a space in it, while an unquoted \u003ccode>${hosts[@]}\u003c/code> splits it. Associative arrays need \u003ccode>declare -A\u003c/code> and Bash 4 or newer, which excludes the default Bash on macOS.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reading-data-safely\">Reading data safely\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"mapfile -t lines &lt; &lt;(grep -v '^#' servers.txt)\necho &quot;read ${#lines[@]} lines&quot;\n\nwhile IFS=, read -r name ip role; do\n  printf '%s has %s (%s)\\n' &quot;$name&quot; &quot;$ip&quot; &quot;$role&quot;\ndone &lt; inventory.csv\n\ndiff &lt;(sort old.txt) &lt;(sort new.txt)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">mapfile\u003c/span> -t lines &lt; &lt;(grep -v \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;^#&#x27;\u003c/span> servers.txt)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;read \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${#lines[@]}\u003c/span> lines&quot;\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">while\u003c/span> IFS=, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">read\u003c/span> -r name ip role; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">do\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">printf\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;%s has %s (%s)\\n&#x27;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$name\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$ip\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$role\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">done\u003c/span> &lt; inventory.csv\n\ndiff &lt;(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sort\u003c/span> old.txt) &lt;(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sort\u003c/span> new.txt)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>&lt;(command)\u003c/code> is \u003cstrong>process substitution\u003c/strong>: it makes a command&#39;s output look like a file. \u003ccode>while read\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>IFS=\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>-r\u003c/code> preserves leading whitespace and backslashes. Reading with \u003ccode>for line in $(cat file)\u003c/code> splits on every space and is almost always a bug.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"here-documents\">Here-documents\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"cat &gt; /etc/myapp/app.conf &lt;&lt;EOF\nlisten = ${PORT:-8080}\nenvironment = ${ENVIRONMENT:?ENVIRONMENT must be set}\nEOF\n\ncat &lt;&lt;'EOF'\nThe quotes around the delimiter stop expansion, so $HOME prints literally.\nEOF\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cat\u003c/span> &gt; /etc/myapp/app.conf &lt;&lt;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">EOF\nlisten = ${PORT:-8080}\nenvironment = ${ENVIRONMENT:?ENVIRONMENT must be set}\nEOF\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cat\u003c/span> &lt;&lt;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;EOF&#x27;\u003c/span>\nThe quotes around the delimiter stop expansion, so \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$HOME\u003c/span> prints literally.\nEOF\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>An unquoted delimiter allows variable expansion. Quoting the delimiter prints the text as written, which is what you want when generating scripts or Kubernetes manifests that contain dollar signs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"parameter-expansion-you-will-use-daily\">Parameter expansion you will use daily\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"file=&quot;/var/log/app/server.log.gz&quot;\necho &quot;${file##*/}&quot;        # server.log.gz  (basename)\necho &quot;${file%/*}&quot;         # /var/log/app   (dirname)\necho &quot;${file%.gz}&quot;        # strip suffix\nname=&quot;${1:-world}&quot;        # default value\necho &quot;${name^^}&quot;          # uppercase\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">file=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;/var/log/app/server.log.gz&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${file##*/}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># server.log.gz  (basename)\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${file%/*}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>         \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># /var/log/app   (dirname)\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${file%.gz}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># strip suffix\u003c/span>\nname=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${1:-world}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># default value\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${name^^}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># uppercase\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-filename-with-a-space\">Incident: the filename with a space\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A cleanup script loops \u003ccode>for f in $(ls /data/uploads)\u003c/code> and runs \u003ccode>rm $f\u003c/code>. A customer uploads a file named \u003ccode>report 2024.pdf\u003c/code>. The loop deletes \u003ccode>report\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>2024.pdf\u003c/code> as two separate names, and in another run an empty variable turns \u003ccode>rm $f\u003c/code> into a command with no operand.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>Word splitting is the cause. Use globs, quote every expansion and guard with a test:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"for f in /data/uploads/*; do\n  [ -e &quot;$f&quot; ] || continue\n  if [ &quot;$(find &quot;$f&quot; -mtime +30 | wc -l)&quot; -gt 0 ]; then\n    echo &quot;removing: $f&quot;\n    rm -- &quot;$f&quot;\n  fi\ndone\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> f \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> /data/uploads/*; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">do\u003c/span>\n  [ -e \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$f\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> ] || \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">continue\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> [ \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">$(find \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$f\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> -mtime +30 | wc -l)\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> -gt 0 ]; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">then\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;removing: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$f\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">rm\u003c/span> -- \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$f\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">fi\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">done\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>rm --\u003c/code> stops option parsing so a file named \u003ccode>-rf\u003c/code> is treated as a name. Run \u003ccode>shellcheck script.sh\u003c/code> as part of your pipeline; it catches unquoted variables like this automatically.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Looping over \u003ccode>$(ls)\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>$(cat file)\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Unquoted \u003ccode>$var\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>${array[@]}\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using associative arrays on systems with Bash 3\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting \u003ccode>shift\u003c/code> after getopts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Trusting \u003ccode>echo\u003c/code> with data that starts with a dash; prefer \u003ccode>printf\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"options-with-getopts","text":"Options with getopts"},{"id":"arrays","text":"Arrays"},{"id":"reading-data-safely","text":"Reading data safely"},{"id":"here-documents","text":"Here-documents"},{"id":"parameter-expansion-you-will-use-daily","text":"Parameter expansion you will use daily"},{"id":"incident-the-filename-with-a-space","text":"Incident: the filename with a space"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"GNU Bash reference manual","u":"https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/bash.html"},{"t":"ShellCheck","u":"https://www.shellcheck.net/"},{"t":"POSIX shell command language","u":"https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799/utilities/V3_chap02.html"}],"text":"Once a script is more than a dozen lines, you need real argument handling, data structures and ways to generate files. Bash has all of these, with a few sharp edges you should know about. What you will learn Parsing flags with getopts and writing a usage message Indexed and associative arrays, and quoting them correctly Here-documents for config files and messages Process substitution, mapfile and parameter expansion Options with getopts #!/usr/bin/env bash set -euo pipefail usage () { echo \"usage: $0 [-e env] [-n] [-v] service...\" >&2; exit 2; } env = \"staging\" ; dry_run=0; verbose=0 while getopts \":e:nvh\" opt; do case \" $opt \" in e) env = \" $OPTARG \" ;; n) dry_run=1 ;; v) verbose=1 ;; h) usage ;; :) echo \"option - $OPTARG needs a value\" >&2; usage ;; \\?) echo \"unknown option - $OPTARG \" >&2; usage ;; esac done shift $((OPTIND - 1 )) [ \" $# \" -ge 1 ] || usage for svc in \" $@ \" ; do echo \"deploying $svc to $env (dry-run= $dry_run )\" done A trailing colon after a letter in the option string means the option takes a value, and a leading colon turns on silent error handling so you print your own messages. After the loop, shift removes the parsed options so \"$@\" holds only the remaining arguments. Arrays hosts=(web-1 web-2 \"db 1\" ) hosts+=(cache-1) echo \" ${#hosts[@]} hosts, first is ${hosts[0]} \" for h in \" ${hosts[@]} \" ; do echo \"checking: $h \" done declare -A port=([web]=8080 [api]=9000) port[metrics]=9100 for name in \" ${!port[@]} \" ; do echo \" $name uses port ${port[$name]} \" done The quoting is the whole point: \"${hosts[@]}\" expands to one word per element and preserves the element with a space in it, while an unquoted ${hosts[@]} splits it. Associative arrays need declare -A and Bash 4 or newer, which excludes the default Bash on macOS. Reading data safely mapfile -t lines \u003c \u003c(grep -v &#x27;^#&#x27; servers.txt) echo \"read ${#lines[@]} lines\" while IFS=, read -r name ip role; do printf &#x27;%s has %s (%s)\\n&#x27; \" $name \" \" $ip \" \" $role \" done \u003c inventory.csv diff \u003c( sort old.txt) \u003c( sort new.txt) \u003c(command) is process substitution : it makes a command&#39;s output look like a file. while read with IFS= and -r preserves leading whitespace and backslashes. Reading with for line in $(cat file) splits on every space and is almost always a bug. Here-documents cat > /etc/myapp/app.conf \u003c\u003c EOF listen = ${PORT:-8080} environment = ${ENVIRONMENT:?ENVIRONMENT must be set} EOF cat \u003c\u003c &#x27;EOF&#x27; The quotes around the delimiter stop expansion, so $HOME prints literally. EOF An unquoted delimiter allows variable expansion. Quoting the delimiter prints the text as written, which is what you want when generating scripts or Kubernetes manifests that contain dollar signs. Parameter expansion you will use daily file= \"/var/log/app/server.log.gz\" echo \" ${file##*/} \" # server.log.gz (basename) echo \" ${file%/*} \" # /var/log/app (dirname) echo \" ${file%.gz} \" # strip suffix name= \" ${1:-world} \" # default value echo \" ${name^^} \" # uppercase Incident: the filename with a space Incident A cleanup script loops for f in $(ls /data/uploads) and runs rm $f . A customer uploads a file named report 2024.pdf . The loop deletes report and 2024.pdf as two separate names, and in another run an empty variable turns rm $f into a command with no operand. Word splitting is the cause. Use globs, quote every expansion and guard with a test: for f in /data/uploads/*; do [ -e \" $f \" ] || continue if [ \" $(find \" $f \" -mtime +30 | wc -l) \" -gt 0 ]; then echo \"removing: $f \" rm -- \" $f \" fi done rm -- stops option parsing so a file named -rf is treated as a name. Run shellcheck script.sh as part of your pipeline; it catches unquoted variables like this automatically. Common mistakes Looping over $(ls) or $(cat file) Unquoted $var and ${array[@]} Using associative arrays on systems with Bash 3 Forgetting shift after getopts Trusting echo with data that starts with a dash; prefer printf"},"bash/apis-and-json":{"html":"\u003cp>Modern infrastructure is controlled through APIs: cloud providers, Kubernetes, monitoring systems and chat tools. \u003ccode>curl\u003c/code> talks to them, and \u003ccode>jq\u003c/code> turns their JSON responses into exactly the fields you need.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The curl flags you need for scripts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Selecting, filtering and reshaping JSON with jq\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Checking HTTP status codes and retrying\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Passing credentials safely\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"curl-for-scripts\">curl for scripts\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"curl -sS https://api.example.com/v1/health\ncurl -sS -o /dev/null -w &quot;%{http_code} %{time_total}s\\n&quot; https://api.example.com/v1/health\ncurl -fsS -m 10 --retry 3 --retry-delay 2 https://api.example.com/v1/servers\ncurl -fsS -X POST https://api.example.com/v1/servers -H &quot;Content-Type: application/json&quot; -d '{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;web-3&quot;,&quot;size&quot;:&quot;small&quot;}'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -sS https://api.example.com/v1/health\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -sS -o /dev/null -w \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;%{http_code} %{time_total}s\\n&quot;\u003c/span> https://api.example.com/v1/health\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -fsS -m 10 --retry 3 --retry-delay 2 https://api.example.com/v1/servers\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -fsS -X POST https://api.example.com/v1/servers -H \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Content-Type: application/json&quot;\u003c/span> -d \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;web-3&quot;,&quot;size&quot;:&quot;small&quot;}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The flags to remember: \u003ccode>-s\u003c/code> silences the progress meter, \u003ccode>-S\u003c/code> still shows errors, \u003ccode>-f\u003c/code> makes curl exit non-zero for HTTP 400 and above, \u003ccode>-m\u003c/code> sets a time limit, and \u003ccode>-w\u003c/code> prints details such as the status code. Without \u003ccode>-f\u003c/code>, a 500 response with an HTML error page looks like success to your script.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"credentials\">Credentials\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Never write tokens on the command line, because they appear in the process list and shell history. Read them from the environment, a file or a secret manager.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"#!/usr/bin/env bash\nset -euo pipefail\n: &quot;${API_TOKEN:?set API_TOKEN}&quot;\n\ncurl -fsS -m 10 \\\n  -H &quot;Authorization: Bearer ${API_TOKEN}&quot; \\\n  https://api.example.com/v1/servers\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-meta\">#!/usr/bin/env bash\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span> -euo pipefail\n: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${API_TOKEN:?set API_TOKEN}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\n\ncurl -fsS -m 10 \\\n  -H \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Authorization: Bearer \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${API_TOKEN}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> \\\n  https://api.example.com/v1/servers\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>With \u003ccode>--config\u003c/code> curl can also read headers from a file or standard input, which keeps them out of \u003ccode>ps\u003c/code> output.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"jq-basics\">jq basics\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"echo '{&quot;items&quot;:[{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;web-1&quot;,&quot;cpu&quot;:0.91,&quot;tags&quot;:[&quot;prod&quot;]},{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;db-1&quot;,&quot;cpu&quot;:0.40,&quot;tags&quot;:[&quot;prod&quot;,&quot;db&quot;]}]}' &gt; servers.json\njq '.items[].name' servers.json\njq -r '.items[] | select(.cpu &gt; 0.8) | .name' servers.json\njq -r '.items[] | [.name, .cpu] | @tsv' servers.json\njq '.items | map({host: .name, hot: (.cpu &gt; 0.8)})' servers.json\njq '[.items[] | select(.tags | index(&quot;db&quot;))] | length' servers.json\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{&quot;items&quot;:[{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;web-1&quot;,&quot;cpu&quot;:0.91,&quot;tags&quot;:[&quot;prod&quot;]},{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;db-1&quot;,&quot;cpu&quot;:0.40,&quot;tags&quot;:[&quot;prod&quot;,&quot;db&quot;]}]}&#x27;\u003c/span> &gt; servers.json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>jq \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;.items[].name&#x27;\u003c/span> servers.json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>jq -r \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;.items[] | select(.cpu &gt; 0.8) | .name&#x27;\u003c/span> servers.json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>jq -r \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;.items[] | [.name, .cpu] | @tsv&#x27;\u003c/span> servers.json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>jq \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;.items | map({host: .name, hot: (.cpu &gt; 0.8)})&#x27;\u003c/span> servers.json\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>jq \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;[.items[] | select(.tags | index(&quot;db&quot;))] | length&#x27;\u003c/span> servers.json\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>-r\u003c/code> outputs raw strings without quotes, which is what shell variables want. \u003ccode>select()\u003c/code> filters, \u003ccode>map()\u003c/code> transforms every element, and \u003ccode>@tsv\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>@csv\u003c/code> format rows for reports. Use \u003ccode>jq -e\u003c/code> in conditions so a false or null result gives a non-zero exit code.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"building-json-safely\">Building JSON safely\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Never assemble JSON by gluing strings together: a quote in a value breaks the document.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"jq -n --arg name &quot;web-3&quot; --argjson replicas 3 '{name: $name, replicas: $replicas}'\njq --arg v &quot;1.8.2&quot; '.version = $v' config.json &gt; config.new &amp;&amp; mv config.new config.json\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>jq -n --arg name \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;web-3&quot;\u003c/span> --argjson replicas 3 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{name: $name, replicas: $replicas}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>jq --arg v \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;1.8.2&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;.version = $v&#x27;\u003c/span> config.json &gt; config.new &amp;&amp; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">mv\u003c/span> config.new config.json\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"pagination-and-status-checks\">Pagination and status checks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"#!/usr/bin/env bash\nset -euo pipefail\nurl=&quot;https://api.example.com/v1/servers?limit=100&quot;\nwhile [ -n &quot;$url&quot; ]; do\n  resp=$(curl -fsS -m 10 -H &quot;Authorization: Bearer ${API_TOKEN}&quot; &quot;$url&quot;)\n  echo &quot;$resp&quot; | jq -r '.items[].name'\n  url=$(echo &quot;$resp&quot; | jq -r '.next // empty')\ndone\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-meta\">#!/usr/bin/env bash\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span> -euo pipefail\nurl=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;https://api.example.com/v1/servers?limit=100&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">while\u003c/span> [ -n \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$url\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> ]; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">do\u003c/span>\n  resp=$(curl -fsS -m 10 -H \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Authorization: Bearer \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${API_TOKEN}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$url\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>)\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$resp\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> | jq -r \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;.items[].name&#x27;\u003c/span>\n  url=$(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$resp\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> | jq -r \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;.next // empty&#x27;\u003c/span>)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">done\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The \u003ccode>// empty\u003c/code> fallback prevents the literal string \u003ccode>null\u003c/code> ending up in the variable when there is no next page, so the loop ends cleanly.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-pipeline-that-deployed-on-a-502\">Incident: the pipeline that deployed on a 502\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A deploy script runs \u003ccode>curl https://api/deploy/status | jq -r .state\u003c/code>. The API returns a 502 HTML page, jq fails to parse it, and because the pipeline lacks \u003ccode>pipefail\u003c/code> the step still exits 0. The pipeline then proceeds as if the deployment had succeeded.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>Two defects: curl did not fail on HTTP errors, and the pipeline hid the failure. The robust version:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"set -euo pipefail\nstate=$(curl -fsS -m 15 --retry 3 &quot;https://api.example.com/deploy/status&quot; | jq -er '.state')\n[ &quot;$state&quot; = &quot;ready&quot; ] || { echo &quot;deployment is $state&quot; &gt;&amp;2; exit 1; }\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span> -euo pipefail\nstate=$(curl -fsS -m 15 --retry 3 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;https://api.example.com/deploy/status&quot;\u003c/span> | jq -er \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;.state&#x27;\u003c/span>)\n[ \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$state\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ready&quot;\u003c/span> ] || { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;deployment is \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$state\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> &gt;&amp;2; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exit\u003c/span> 1; }\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>-f\u003c/code> fails on 4xx and 5xx, \u003ccode>pipefail\u003c/code> propagates the error from the first command in the pipe, and \u003ccode>jq -e\u003c/code> fails when the field is missing.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using curl without \u003ccode>-f\u003c/code>, so errors look like success\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Putting tokens on the command line\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Building JSON bodies with string concatenation\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting \u003ccode>-r\u003c/code>, leaving quotes in shell variables\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring pagination and seeing only the first page\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"curl-for-scripts","text":"curl for scripts"},{"id":"credentials","text":"Credentials"},{"id":"jq-basics","text":"jq basics"},{"id":"building-json-safely","text":"Building JSON safely"},{"id":"pagination-and-status-checks","text":"Pagination and status checks"},{"id":"incident-the-pipeline-that-deployed-on-a-502","text":"Incident: the pipeline that deployed on a 502"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"curl documentation","u":"https://curl.se/docs/manpage.html"},{"t":"Everything curl","u":"https://everything.curl.dev/"},{"t":"jq manual","u":"https://jqlang.org/manual/"}],"text":"Modern infrastructure is controlled through APIs: cloud providers, Kubernetes, monitoring systems and chat tools. curl talks to them, and jq turns their JSON responses into exactly the fields you need. What you will learn The curl flags you need for scripts Selecting, filtering and reshaping JSON with jq Checking HTTP status codes and retrying Passing credentials safely curl for scripts $ curl -sS https://api.example.com/v1/health $ curl -sS -o /dev/null -w \"%{http_code} %{time_total}s\\n\" https://api.example.com/v1/health $ curl -fsS -m 10 --retry 3 --retry-delay 2 https://api.example.com/v1/servers $ curl -fsS -X POST https://api.example.com/v1/servers -H \"Content-Type: application/json\" -d &#x27;{\"name\":\"web-3\",\"size\":\"small\"}&#x27; The flags to remember: -s silences the progress meter, -S still shows errors, -f makes curl exit non-zero for HTTP 400 and above, -m sets a time limit, and -w prints details such as the status code. Without -f , a 500 response with an HTML error page looks like success to your script. Credentials Never write tokens on the command line, because they appear in the process list and shell history. Read them from the environment, a file or a secret manager. #!/usr/bin/env bash set -euo pipefail : \" ${API_TOKEN:?set API_TOKEN} \" curl -fsS -m 10 \\ -H \"Authorization: Bearer ${API_TOKEN} \" \\ https://api.example.com/v1/servers With --config curl can also read headers from a file or standard input, which keeps them out of ps output. jq basics $ echo &#x27;{\"items\":[{\"name\":\"web-1\",\"cpu\":0.91,\"tags\":[\"prod\"]},{\"name\":\"db-1\",\"cpu\":0.40,\"tags\":[\"prod\",\"db\"]}]}&#x27; > servers.json $ jq &#x27;.items[].name&#x27; servers.json $ jq -r &#x27;.items[] | select(.cpu > 0.8) | .name&#x27; servers.json $ jq -r &#x27;.items[] | [.name, .cpu] | @tsv&#x27; servers.json $ jq &#x27;.items | map({host: .name, hot: (.cpu > 0.8)})&#x27; servers.json $ jq &#x27;[.items[] | select(.tags | index(\"db\"))] | length&#x27; servers.json -r outputs raw strings without quotes, which is what shell variables want. select() filters, map() transforms every element, and @tsv or @csv format rows for reports. Use jq -e in conditions so a false or null result gives a non-zero exit code. Building JSON safely Never assemble JSON by gluing strings together: a quote in a value breaks the document. $ jq -n --arg name \"web-3\" --argjson replicas 3 &#x27;{name: $name, replicas: $replicas}&#x27; $ jq --arg v \"1.8.2\" &#x27;.version = $v&#x27; config.json > config.new && mv config.new config.json Pagination and status checks #!/usr/bin/env bash set -euo pipefail url= \"https://api.example.com/v1/servers?limit=100\" while [ -n \" $url \" ]; do resp=$(curl -fsS -m 10 -H \"Authorization: Bearer ${API_TOKEN} \" \" $url \" ) echo \" $resp \" | jq -r &#x27;.items[].name&#x27; url=$( echo \" $resp \" | jq -r &#x27;.next // empty&#x27; ) done The // empty fallback prevents the literal string null ending up in the variable when there is no next page, so the loop ends cleanly. Incident: the pipeline that deployed on a 502 Incident A deploy script runs curl https://api/deploy/status | jq -r .state . The API returns a 502 HTML page, jq fails to parse it, and because the pipeline lacks pipefail the step still exits 0. The pipeline then proceeds as if the deployment had succeeded. Two defects: curl did not fail on HTTP errors, and the pipeline hid the failure. The robust version: set -euo pipefail state=$(curl -fsS -m 15 --retry 3 \"https://api.example.com/deploy/status\" | jq -er &#x27;.state&#x27; ) [ \" $state \" = \"ready\" ] || { echo \"deployment is $state \" >&2; exit 1; } -f fails on 4xx and 5xx, pipefail propagates the error from the first command in the pipe, and jq -e fails when the field is missing. Common mistakes Using curl without -f , so errors look like success Putting tokens on the command line Building JSON bodies with string concatenation Forgetting -r , leaving quotes in shell variables Ignoring pagination and seeing only the first page"},"bash/safe-automation":{"html":"\u003cp>A backup script has run every night for a year. One night the disk fills, the dump fails halfway, and the script keeps going. It compresses a truncated file, reports success and deletes yesterday&#39;s good backup. Automation that fails silently is worse than no automation. This lesson covers the habits that make scripts safe to run unattended.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Strict mode: \u003ccode>set -euo pipefail\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cleaning up with \u003ccode>trap\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Preventing two copies from running at once\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Scheduling with cron and systemd timers\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"strict-mode\">Strict mode\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"#!/usr/bin/env bash\nset -euo pipefail\nIFS=$'\\n\\t'\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-meta\">#!/usr/bin/env bash\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span> -euo pipefail\nIFS=$\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;\\n\\t&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Option\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>What it does\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>-e\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Exit when a command fails\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>-u\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Treat unset variables as errors\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>-o pipefail\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A pipeline fails if \u003cem>any\u003c/em> stage fails, not just the last\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Without \u003ccode>pipefail\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>pg_dump mydb | gzip &gt; backup.gz\u003c/code> reports success even when \u003ccode>pg_dump\u003c/code> fails, because \u003ccode>gzip\u003c/code> succeeds. With it, the script stops.\u003c/p>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-note\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Note\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>set -e\u003c/code> has exceptions: it does not trigger inside an \u003ccode>if\u003c/code> condition or on the left of \u003ccode>&amp;&amp;\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>||\u003c/code>. Strict mode is a safety net, not a replacement for checking important results yourself.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003ch2 id=\"clean-up-with-trap\">Clean up with trap\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003ccode>trap\u003c/code> runs a command when the script exits or receives a signal. Use it to remove temporary files whatever happens.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"tmp=&quot;$(mktemp -d)&quot;\ncleanup() { rm -rf -- &quot;$tmp&quot;; }\ntrap cleanup EXIT                 # runs on normal exit, on error, and on Ctrl+C\n\npg_dump &quot;$DB_NAME&quot; | gzip &gt; &quot;$tmp/db.sql.gz&quot;\nmv &quot;$tmp/db.sql.gz&quot; &quot;/backups/db-$(date +%F).sql.gz&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">tmp=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">$(mktemp -d)\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-function\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-title\">cleanup\u003c/span>\u003c/span>() { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">rm\u003c/span> -rf -- \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$tmp\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>; }\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">trap\u003c/span> cleanup EXIT                 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># runs on normal exit, on error, and on Ctrl+C\u003c/span>\n\npg_dump \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$DB_NAME\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> | gzip &gt; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$tmp\u003c/span>/db.sql.gz&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">mv\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$tmp\u003c/span>/db.sql.gz&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;/backups/db-\u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">$(date +%F)\u003c/span>.sql.gz&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Write to a temporary location and \u003cstrong>move into place at the end\u003c/strong>. A move on the same filesystem is atomic, so readers never see a half-written file.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"one-run-at-a-time\">One run at a time\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>If a job takes longer than its schedule interval, two copies run together and can corrupt data. \u003ccode>flock\u003c/code> takes a lock and refuses to start a second copy.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"exec 9&gt;/var/lock/nightly-backup.lock\nflock -n 9 || { echo &quot;another backup is running&quot; &gt;&amp;2; exit 0; }\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exec\u003c/span> 9&gt;/var/lock/nightly-backup.lock\nflock -n 9 || { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;another backup is running&quot;\u003c/span> &gt;&amp;2; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exit\u003c/span> 0; }\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The lock is released automatically when the script exits, even after a crash.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"scheduling\">Scheduling\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>cron\u003c/strong> is the classic scheduler. Edit with \u003ccode>crontab -e\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# minute hour day-of-month month day-of-week  command\n15 2 * * *  /usr/local/bin/nightly-backup.sh &gt;&gt; /var/log/nightly-backup.log 2&gt;&amp;1\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># minute hour day-of-month month day-of-week  command\u003c/span>\n15 2 * * *  /usr/local/bin/nightly-backup.sh &gt;&gt; /var/log/nightly-backup.log 2&gt;&amp;1\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Cron runs with a minimal environment, so use full paths and set what the script needs. Capture output, because cron mails or drops it otherwise.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>systemd timers\u003c/strong> do the same job with better logging and dependencies. A timer triggers a service unit.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"ini\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# /etc/systemd/system/backup.timer\n[Unit]\nDescription=Nightly backup\n\n[Timer]\nOnCalendar=*-*-* 02:15:00\nPersistent=true\nRandomizedDelaySec=300\n\n[Install]\nWantedBy=timers.target\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># /etc/systemd/system/backup.timer\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-section\">[Unit]\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Description\u003c/span>=Nightly backup\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-section\">[Timer]\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">OnCalendar\u003c/span>=*-*-* \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">02\u003c/span>:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">15\u003c/span>:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">00\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">Persistent\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">RandomizedDelaySec\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">300\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-section\">[Install]\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">WantedBy\u003c/span>=timers.target\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>Persistent=true\u003c/code> runs a missed job after the machine was off. Enable with \u003ccode>systemctl enable --now backup.timer\u003c/code>, list with \u003ccode>systemctl list-timers\u003c/code>, and read output with \u003ccode>journalctl -u backup.service\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-backup-that-deleted-the-last-good-copy\">Incident: the backup that deleted the last good copy\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>The nightly job ran \u003ccode>pg_dump | gzip &gt; latest.sql.gz\u003c/code>, then \u003ccode>find /backups -mtime +7 -delete\u003c/code>. The database host was out of space, \u003ccode>pg_dump\u003c/code> failed, and \u003ccode>latest.sql.gz\u003c/code> contained a few kilobytes. Cleanup then removed older files. After a week there was no usable backup.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>A safer version applies every habit from this lesson:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"#!/usr/bin/env bash\nset -euo pipefail\n\nbackup_dir=&quot;${BACKUP_DIR:?BACKUP_DIR must be set}&quot;\ntmp=&quot;$(mktemp -d)&quot;; trap 'rm -rf -- &quot;$tmp&quot;' EXIT\nexec 9&gt;/var/lock/nightly-backup.lock; flock -n 9 || exit 0\n\nout=&quot;$backup_dir/db-$(date +%F).sql.gz&quot;\npg_dump &quot;$DB_NAME&quot; | gzip &gt; &quot;$tmp/dump.gz&quot;\n\n# validate before replacing anything\ngzip -t &quot;$tmp/dump.gz&quot;\n[[ &quot;$(stat -c %s &quot;$tmp/dump.gz&quot;)&quot; -gt 1048576 ]] || { echo &quot;dump suspiciously small&quot; &gt;&amp;2; exit 1; }\n\nmv &quot;$tmp/dump.gz&quot; &quot;$out&quot;\nfind &quot;$backup_dir&quot; -name 'db-*.sql.gz' -mtime +7 -delete   # only after a verified new backup\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-meta\">#!/usr/bin/env bash\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span> -euo pipefail\n\nbackup_dir=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${BACKUP_DIR:?BACKUP_DIR must be set}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\ntmp=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">$(mktemp -d)\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">trap\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;rm -rf -- &quot;$tmp&quot;&#x27;\u003c/span> EXIT\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exec\u003c/span> 9&gt;/var/lock/nightly-backup.lock; flock -n 9 || \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exit\u003c/span> 0\n\nout=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$backup_dir\u003c/span>/db-\u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">$(date +%F)\u003c/span>.sql.gz&quot;\u003c/span>\npg_dump \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$DB_NAME\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> | gzip &gt; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$tmp\u003c/span>/dump.gz&quot;\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># validate before replacing anything\u003c/span>\ngzip -t \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$tmp\u003c/span>/dump.gz&quot;\u003c/span>\n[[ \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">$(stat -c %s \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$tmp\u003c/span>/dump.gz&quot;\u003c/span>)\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> -gt 1048576 ]] || { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;dump suspiciously small&quot;\u003c/span> &gt;&amp;2; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exit\u003c/span> 1; }\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">mv\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$tmp\u003c/span>/dump.gz&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$out\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\nfind \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$backup_dir\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> -name \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;db-*.sql.gz&#x27;\u003c/span> -mtime +7 -delete   \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># only after a verified new backup\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>If \u003ccode>pg_dump\u003c/code> fails, \u003ccode>pipefail\u003c/code> stops the script before any deletion. The size and integrity checks catch truncated output. Old backups are removed only after a new one has been verified. Finally, a \u003cstrong>restore test\u003c/strong> matters more than any check: schedule a monthly job that actually restores the latest backup into a scratch database.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"start&gt;check;start&gt;dry;check&gt;act;dry&gt;act;act&gt;end\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Where a safe script protects you\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"start\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Start\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"check\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Validate input\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"dry\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Dry run\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"act\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Do the work\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"end\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Cleanup and exit code\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Start to Validate input; 2, Start to Dry run; 3, Validate input to Do the work; 4, Dry run to Do the work; 5, Do the work to Cleanup and exit code.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"start\">\u003cdt>Start \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The script begins with set -euo pipefail and a trap for cleanup.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Without it, an error in the middle is ignored and the script goes on.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"check\">\u003cdt>Validate input \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Check that required variables are set and paths are not empty.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> An empty variable in rm -rf $DIR/ can become rm -rf /.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"dry\">\u003cdt>Dry run \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Print the actions first with a --dry-run flag.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Skipping it means the first real run is also the first test.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"act\">\u003cdt>Do the work \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Use quoted variables, a lock file and clear logging.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Two copies running at once can corrupt data without a lock.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"end\">\u003cdt>Cleanup and exit code \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The trap removes temp files and returns a meaningful code.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> If the exit code is always 0, the scheduler thinks a failure succeeded.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Cleaning up old data before confirming the new data is good\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Relying on \u003ccode>set -e\u003c/code> and never checking critical results\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cron jobs that depend on \u003ccode>PATH\u003c/code> or variables from your interactive shell\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never testing a restore\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"strict-mode","text":"Strict mode"},{"id":"clean-up-with-trap","text":"Clean up with trap"},{"id":"one-run-at-a-time","text":"One run at a time"},{"id":"scheduling","text":"Scheduling"},{"id":"incident-the-backup-that-deleted-the-last-good-copy","text":"Incident: the backup that deleted the last good copy"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"GNU Bash reference manual","u":"https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/bash.html"},{"t":"ShellCheck","u":"https://www.shellcheck.net/"},{"t":"systemd.timer manual","u":"https://www.freedesktop.org/software/systemd/man/latest/systemd.timer.html"}],"text":"A backup script has run every night for a year. One night the disk fills, the dump fails halfway, and the script keeps going. It compresses a truncated file, reports success and deletes yesterday&#39;s good backup. Automation that fails silently is worse than no automation. This lesson covers the habits that make scripts safe to run unattended. What you will learn Strict mode: set -euo pipefail Cleaning up with trap Preventing two copies from running at once Scheduling with cron and systemd timers Strict mode #!/usr/bin/env bash set -euo pipefail IFS=$ &#x27;\\n\\t&#x27; Option What it does -e Exit when a command fails -u Treat unset variables as errors -o pipefail A pipeline fails if any stage fails, not just the last Without pipefail , pg_dump mydb | gzip > backup.gz reports success even when pg_dump fails, because gzip succeeds. With it, the script stops. Note set -e has exceptions: it does not trigger inside an if condition or on the left of && and || . Strict mode is a safety net, not a replacement for checking important results yourself. Clean up with trap A trap runs a command when the script exits or receives a signal. Use it to remove temporary files whatever happens. tmp= \" $(mktemp -d) \" cleanup () { rm -rf -- \" $tmp \" ; } trap cleanup EXIT # runs on normal exit, on error, and on Ctrl+C pg_dump \" $DB_NAME \" | gzip > \" $tmp /db.sql.gz\" mv \" $tmp /db.sql.gz\" \"/backups/db- $(date +%F) .sql.gz\" Write to a temporary location and move into place at the end . A move on the same filesystem is atomic, so readers never see a half-written file. One run at a time If a job takes longer than its schedule interval, two copies run together and can corrupt data. flock takes a lock and refuses to start a second copy. exec 9>/var/lock/nightly-backup.lock flock -n 9 || { echo \"another backup is running\" >&2; exit 0; } The lock is released automatically when the script exits, even after a crash. Scheduling cron is the classic scheduler. Edit with crontab -e . # minute hour day-of-month month day-of-week command 15 2 * * * /usr/local/bin/nightly-backup.sh >> /var/log/nightly-backup.log 2>&1 Cron runs with a minimal environment, so use full paths and set what the script needs. Capture output, because cron mails or drops it otherwise. systemd timers do the same job with better logging and dependencies. A timer triggers a service unit. # /etc/systemd/system/backup.timer [Unit] Description =Nightly backup [Timer] OnCalendar =*-*-* 02 : 15 : 00 Persistent = true RandomizedDelaySec = 300 [Install] WantedBy =timers.target Persistent=true runs a missed job after the machine was off. Enable with systemctl enable --now backup.timer , list with systemctl list-timers , and read output with journalctl -u backup.service . Incident: the backup that deleted the last good copy Incident The nightly job ran pg_dump | gzip > latest.sql.gz , then find /backups -mtime +7 -delete . The database host was out of space, pg_dump failed, and latest.sql.gz contained a few kilobytes. Cleanup then removed older files. After a week there was no usable backup. A safer version applies every habit from this lesson: #!/usr/bin/env bash set -euo pipefail backup_dir= \" ${BACKUP_DIR:?BACKUP_DIR must be set} \" tmp= \" $(mktemp -d) \" ; trap &#x27;rm -rf -- \"$tmp\"&#x27; EXIT exec 9>/var/lock/nightly-backup.lock; flock -n 9 || exit 0 out= \" $backup_dir /db- $(date +%F) .sql.gz\" pg_dump \" $DB_NAME \" | gzip > \" $tmp /dump.gz\" # validate before replacing anything gzip -t \" $tmp /dump.gz\" [[ \" $(stat -c %s \" $tmp /dump.gz\" ) \" -gt 1048576 ]] || { echo \"dump suspiciously small\" >&2; exit 1; } mv \" $tmp /dump.gz\" \" $out \" find \" $backup_dir \" -name &#x27;db-*.sql.gz&#x27; -mtime +7 -delete # only after a verified new backup If pg_dump fails, pipefail stops the script before any deletion. The size and integrity checks catch truncated output. Old backups are removed only after a new one has been verified. Finally, a restore test matters more than any check: schedule a monthly job that actua"},"bash/arrays-and-parameter-expansion":{"html":"\u003cp>Many shell scripts split strings with \u003ccode>cut\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>awk\u003c/code> or loop over words in a variable, and then break when a value contains a space. Bash has real arrays and a rich set of expansions that handle these jobs safely and without starting extra processes. They make scripts shorter, faster and less fragile.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Indexed and associative arrays\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Looping over arrays safely\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Parameter expansion: defaults, trimming, replacing, lengths and slices\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading command output into arrays\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"indexed-arrays\">Indexed arrays\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"hosts=(web1 web2 &quot;db 1&quot;)\nhosts+=(cache1)\necho &quot;${#hosts[@]}&quot;           # count: 4\necho &quot;${hosts[2]}&quot;            # db 1\nfor h in &quot;${hosts[@]}&quot;; do    # quote and use [@] to keep elements intact\n  echo &quot;checking $h&quot;\ndone\necho &quot;${hosts[@]:1:2}&quot;        # slice: two elements starting at index 1\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">hosts=(web1 web2 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;db 1&quot;\u003c/span>)\nhosts+=(cache1)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${#hosts[@]}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>           \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># count: 4\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${hosts[2]}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># db 1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> h \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${hosts[@]}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">do\u003c/span>    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># quote and use [@] to keep elements intact\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;checking \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$h\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">done\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${hosts[@]:1:2}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># slice: two elements starting at index 1\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The form \u003ccode>&quot;${hosts[@]}&quot;\u003c/code> expands to one word per element, even for elements with spaces. The unquoted form splits on spaces and breaks the list.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reading-output-into-an-array\">Reading output into an array\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"mapfile -t files &lt; &lt;(find /var/log -name '*.log' -mtime -1)\nprintf 'found %d files\\n' &quot;${#files[@]}&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">mapfile\u003c/span> -t files &lt; &lt;(find /var/log -name \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;*.log&#x27;\u003c/span> -mtime -1)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">printf\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;found %d files\\n&#x27;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${#files[@]}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>mapfile\u003c/code> (also \u003ccode>readarray\u003c/code>) reads one line per element and keeps spaces. This is safer than \u003ccode>files=($(find ...))\u003c/code>, which splits on any whitespace.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"associative-arrays\">Associative arrays\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"declare -A owner\nowner[web1]=alice\nowner[db1]=bob\nfor k in &quot;${!owner[@]}&quot;; do echo &quot;$k is owned by ${owner[$k]}&quot;; done\n[[ -v owner[web2] ]] || echo &quot;web2 has no owner&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">declare\u003c/span> -A owner\nowner[web1]=alice\nowner[db1]=bob\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> k \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${!owner[@]}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">do\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$k\u003c/span> is owned by \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${owner[$k]}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">done\u003c/span>\n[[ -v owner[web2] ]] || \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;web2 has no owner&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Use these for lookups such as host to owner or service to port. They need Bash 4 or later, which macOS does not ship by default.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"parameter-expansion\">Parameter expansion\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Form\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Result\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>${name:-default}\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>default\u003c/code> if \u003ccode>name\u003c/code> is unset or empty\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>${name:?message}\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Stop with a message if unset or empty\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>${#name}\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Length of the value\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>${name%.log}\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Remove the shortest match from the end\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>${name##*/}\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Remove the longest match from the start (the file name)\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>${name/old/new}\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Replace the first match\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>${name//old/new}\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Replace all matches\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>${name^^}\u003c/code> / \u003ccode>${name,,}\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Upper or lower case\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"path=/var/log/app/error.log\necho &quot;${path##*/}&quot;      # error.log\necho &quot;${path%/*}&quot;       # /var/log/app\necho &quot;${path%.log}.old&quot; # /var/log/app/error.old\n: &quot;${DB_HOST:?DB_HOST must be set}&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">path=/var/log/app/error.log\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${path##*/}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># error.log\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${path%/*}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>       \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># /var/log/app\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${path%.log}\u003c/span>.old&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># /var/log/app/error.old\u003c/span>\n: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${DB_HOST:?DB_HOST must be set}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"where-to-stop\">Where to stop\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>When a script needs nested data or heavy parsing, use \u003ccode>jq\u003c/code> or move to Python. Arrays are for lists and simple lookups.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> What does this print?\u003c/p>\u003cpre class=\"predict-code\" tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>files=(&quot;a b&quot; c)\nfor f in ${files[@]}; do echo &quot;[$f]&quot;; done\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Three lines: [a], [b] and [c]. The unquoted expansion splits &quot;a b&quot; into two words.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Always use &quot;${files[@]}&quot; with quotes to keep each element as one word.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-backup-that-skipped-half-the-files\">Incident: the backup that skipped half the files\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A backup script reports success but misses files whose names contain spaces.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"files=($(ls /srv/reports)); echo &quot;${#files[@]}&quot;\nls /srv/reports | wc -l\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>files=($(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> /srv/reports)); \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${#files[@]}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">212\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> /srv/reports | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">140\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The script built its list by word-splitting the output of \u003ccode>ls\u003c/code>, so each name with a space became several entries that did not exist. The fix used \u003ccode>mapfile -t files &lt; &lt;(find ... -print0)\u003c/code> style reading with a quoted loop, and the script compared the count of files copied with the count found.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Parsing \u003ccode>ls\u003c/code> output\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Unquoted \u003ccode>${array[@]}\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using associative arrays on shells that lack them\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting \u003ccode>:?\u003c/code> checks for required variables\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"indexed-arrays","text":"Indexed arrays"},{"id":"reading-output-into-an-array","text":"Reading output into an array"},{"id":"associative-arrays","text":"Associative arrays"},{"id":"parameter-expansion","text":"Parameter expansion"},{"id":"where-to-stop","text":"Where to stop"},{"id":"incident-the-backup-that-skipped-half-the-files","text":"Incident: the backup that skipped half the files"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"GNU Bash manual: Arrays","u":"https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/html_node/Arrays.html"},{"t":"GNU Bash manual: Shell parameter expansion","u":"https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/html_node/Shell-Parameter-Expansion.html"}],"text":"Many shell scripts split strings with cut and awk or loop over words in a variable, and then break when a value contains a space. Bash has real arrays and a rich set of expansions that handle these jobs safely and without starting extra processes. They make scripts shorter, faster and less fragile. What you will learn Indexed and associative arrays Looping over arrays safely Parameter expansion: defaults, trimming, replacing, lengths and slices Reading command output into arrays Indexed arrays hosts=(web1 web2 \"db 1\" ) hosts+=(cache1) echo \" ${#hosts[@]} \" # count: 4 echo \" ${hosts[2]} \" # db 1 for h in \" ${hosts[@]} \" ; do # quote and use [@] to keep elements intact echo \"checking $h \" done echo \" ${hosts[@]:1:2} \" # slice: two elements starting at index 1 The form \"${hosts[@]}\" expands to one word per element, even for elements with spaces. The unquoted form splits on spaces and breaks the list. Reading output into an array mapfile -t files \u003c \u003c(find /var/log -name &#x27;*.log&#x27; -mtime -1) printf &#x27;found %d files\\n&#x27; \" ${#files[@]} \" mapfile (also readarray ) reads one line per element and keeps spaces. This is safer than files=($(find ...)) , which splits on any whitespace. Associative arrays declare -A owner owner[web1]=alice owner[db1]=bob for k in \" ${!owner[@]} \" ; do echo \" $k is owned by ${owner[$k]} \" ; done [[ -v owner[web2] ]] || echo \"web2 has no owner\" Use these for lookups such as host to owner or service to port. They need Bash 4 or later, which macOS does not ship by default. Parameter expansion Form Result ${name:-default} default if name is unset or empty ${name:?message} Stop with a message if unset or empty ${#name} Length of the value ${name%.log} Remove the shortest match from the end ${name##*/} Remove the longest match from the start (the file name) ${name/old/new} Replace the first match ${name//old/new} Replace all matches ${name^^} / ${name,,} Upper or lower case path=/var/log/app/error.log echo \" ${path##*/} \" # error.log echo \" ${path%/*} \" # /var/log/app echo \" ${path%.log} .old\" # /var/log/app/error.old : \" ${DB_HOST:?DB_HOST must be set} \" Where to stop When a script needs nested data or heavy parsing, use jq or move to Python. Arrays are for lists and simple lookups. Predict first. What does this print? files=(\"a b\" c) for f in ${files[@]}; do echo \"[$f]\"; done Your guess Answer: Three lines: [a], [b] and [c]. The unquoted expansion splits \"a b\" into two words. Always use \"${files[@]}\" with quotes to keep each element as one word. Incident: the backup that skipped half the files Incident A backup script reports success but misses files whose names contain spaces. $ files=($( ls /srv/reports)); echo \" ${#files[@]} \" 212 $ ls /srv/reports | wc -l 140 The script built its list by word-splitting the output of ls , so each name with a space became several entries that did not exist. The fix used mapfile -t files \u003c \u003c(find ... -print0) style reading with a quoted loop, and the script compared the count of files copied with the count found. Common mistakes Parsing ls output Unquoted ${array[@]} Using associative arrays on shells that lack them Forgetting :? checks for required variables"},"bash/building-and-testing-shell-tools":{"html":"\u003cp>A script that only its author understands becomes a risk the day that person is away. A small tool has a clear interface: options, a help message, meaningful exit codes, and tests. Bash is not the best language for large programs, but a few good habits make shell tools reliable enough to share with a team.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Parsing options with \u003ccode>getopts\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A standard script layout with functions and a \u003ccode>main\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Exit codes and error messages\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Testing with \u003ccode>bats\u003c/code> and checking with \u003ccode>shellcheck\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Making scripts safe to source and to run twice\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"layout\">Layout\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"#!/usr/bin/env bash\nset -Eeuo pipefail\n\nusage() { cat &lt;&lt;'EOF'\nUsage: rotate.sh [-d DIR] [-k DAYS] [-n]\n  -d DIR   directory to clean (required)\n  -k DAYS  keep files newer than DAYS (default 14)\n  -n       dry run, only print what would be removed\nEOF\n}\n\nmain() {\n  local dir=&quot;&quot; keep=14 dry=0 opt\n  while getopts &quot;:d:k:nh&quot; opt; do\n    case $opt in\n      d) dir=$OPTARG ;;\n      k) keep=$OPTARG ;;\n      n) dry=1 ;;\n      h) usage; return 0 ;;\n      :) echo &quot;option -$OPTARG needs a value&quot; &gt;&amp;2; return 64 ;;\n      *) usage &gt;&amp;2; return 64 ;;\n    esac\n  done\n  [[ -n $dir &amp;&amp; -d $dir ]] || { echo &quot;error: -d must be an existing directory&quot; &gt;&amp;2; return 64; }\n  [[ $keep =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] || { echo &quot;error: -k must be a number&quot; &gt;&amp;2; return 64; }\n  if (( dry )); then\n    find &quot;$dir&quot; -type f -mtime &quot;+$keep&quot; -print\n  else\n    find &quot;$dir&quot; -type f -mtime &quot;+$keep&quot; -delete\n  fi\n}\n\nif [[ ${BASH_SOURCE[0]} == &quot;$0&quot; ]]; then main &quot;$@&quot;; fi\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-meta\">#!/usr/bin/env bash\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span> -Eeuo pipefail\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-function\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-title\">usage\u003c/span>\u003c/span>() { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cat\u003c/span> &lt;&lt;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;EOF&#x27;\u003c/span>\nUsage: rotate.sh [-d DIR] [-k DAYS] [-n]\n  -d DIR   directory to clean (required)\n  -k DAYS  keep files newer than DAYS (default 14)\n  -n       dry run, only \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span> what would be removed\nEOF\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-function\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-title\">main\u003c/span>\u003c/span>() {\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">local\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">dir\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;&quot;\u003c/span> keep=14 dry=0 opt\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">while\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">getopts\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;:d:k:nh&quot;\u003c/span> opt; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">do\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">case\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$opt\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span>\n      d) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">dir\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$OPTARG\u003c/span> ;;\n      k) keep=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$OPTARG\u003c/span> ;;\n      n) dry=1 ;;\n      h) usage; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">return\u003c/span> 0 ;;\n      :) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;option -\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$OPTARG\u003c/span> needs a value&quot;\u003c/span> &gt;&amp;2; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">return\u003c/span> 64 ;;\n      *) usage &gt;&amp;2; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">return\u003c/span> 64 ;;\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">esac\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">done\u003c/span>\n  [[ -n \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$dir\u003c/span> &amp;&amp; -d \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$dir\u003c/span> ]] || { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;error: -d must be an existing directory&quot;\u003c/span> &gt;&amp;2; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">return\u003c/span> 64; }\n  [[ \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$keep\u003c/span> =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] || { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;error: -k must be a number&quot;\u003c/span> &gt;&amp;2; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">return\u003c/span> 64; }\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> (( dry )); \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">then\u003c/span>\n    find \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$dir\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> -\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">type\u003c/span> f -mtime \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;+\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$keep\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> -\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">else\u003c/span>\n    find \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$dir\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> -\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">type\u003c/span> f -mtime \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;+\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$keep\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> -delete\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">fi\u003c/span>\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> [[ \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${BASH_SOURCE[0]}\u003c/span> == \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$0\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> ]]; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">then\u003c/span> main \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$@\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">fi\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Putting logic in functions and calling \u003ccode>main\u003c/code> only when the file is run (not sourced) lets tests load the functions without running the tool.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"exit-codes-and-messages\">Exit codes and messages\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Use 0 for success, 1 for a general failure and 64 for a usage error (the convention from \u003ccode>sysexits\u003c/code>). Send errors to standard error (\u003ccode>&gt;&amp;2\u003c/code>) and normal output to standard output, so pipelines keep working. Make the message say what was wrong and what the user can do.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"tests-with-bats\">Tests with bats\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"#!/usr/bin/env bats\n\nsetup() { TMP=$(mktemp -d); touch -d '30 days ago' &quot;$TMP/old.log&quot;; touch &quot;$TMP/new.log&quot;; }\nteardown() { rm -rf &quot;$TMP&quot;; }\n\n@test &quot;dry run lists old files and keeps them&quot; {\n  run ./rotate.sh -d &quot;$TMP&quot; -k 14 -n\n  [ &quot;$status&quot; -eq 0 ]\n  [[ &quot;$output&quot; == *old.log* ]]\n  [ -e &quot;$TMP/old.log&quot; ]\n}\n\n@test &quot;missing directory is a usage error&quot; {\n  run ./rotate.sh -d /nope\n  [ &quot;$status&quot; -eq 64 ]\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">#!/usr/bin/env bats\n\nsetup() { TMP=$(mktemp -d); touch -d &#x27;30 days ago&#x27; &quot;$TMP/old.log&quot;; touch &quot;$TMP/new.log&quot;; }\nteardown() { rm -rf &quot;$TMP&quot;; }\n\n@test &quot;dry run lists old files and keeps them&quot; {\n  run ./rotate.sh -d &quot;$TMP&quot; -k 14 -n\n  [ &quot;$status&quot; -eq 0 ]\n  [[ &quot;$output&quot; == *old.log* ]]\n  [ -e &quot;$TMP/old.log&quot; ]\n}\n\n@test &quot;missing directory is a usage error&quot; {\n  run ./rotate.sh -d /nope\n  [ &quot;$status&quot; -eq 64 ]\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Run \u003ccode>shellcheck rotate.sh\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>bats tests/\u003c/code> in your pipeline. Use a temporary directory for test data and never point tests at real paths.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"idempotence-and-safety\">Idempotence and safety\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A tool you may run twice should give the same result the second time. Prefer \u003ccode>mkdir -p\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>ln -sfn\u003c/code>, and checks such as &quot;does it exist&quot; before changes. Provide a dry-run option for anything destructive, and print what you are about to do.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A script is run with an option it does not know. What should it do?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Print a usage message to standard error and exit with a non-zero code such as 64.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Silent success hides mistakes. A clear message and a non-zero exit let callers and pipelines react.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-cleanup-that-ran-with-the-wrong-variable\">Incident: the cleanup that ran with the wrong variable\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A cleanup script deletes the wrong directory because a path variable was empty after a typo in an option name.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"./clean.sh --dri /data/tmp\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>./clean.sh --dri /data/tmp\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">removing files under /\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The tool ignored unknown options and fell back to an empty directory value. The rewrite validated every option, required \u003ccode>-d\u003c/code> to be an existing directory, refused to run on \u003ccode>/\u003c/code>, offered a dry run, and had tests for each of these cases.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>No validation of required options\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Mixing messages and results on standard output\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Tests that touch real directories\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No dry-run mode for destructive tools\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"layout","text":"Layout"},{"id":"exit-codes-and-messages","text":"Exit codes and messages"},{"id":"tests-with-bats","text":"Tests with bats"},{"id":"idempotence-and-safety","text":"Idempotence and safety"},{"id":"incident-the-cleanup-that-ran-with-the-wrong-variable","text":"Incident: the cleanup that ran with the wrong variable"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"ShellCheck","u":"https://www.shellcheck.net/"},{"t":"GNU Bash manual: Bourne shell builtins (getopts)","u":"https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/html_node/Bourne-Shell-Builtins.html"}],"text":"A script that only its author understands becomes a risk the day that person is away. A small tool has a clear interface: options, a help message, meaningful exit codes, and tests. Bash is not the best language for large programs, but a few good habits make shell tools reliable enough to share with a team. What you will learn Parsing options with getopts A standard script layout with functions and a main Exit codes and error messages Testing with bats and checking with shellcheck Making scripts safe to source and to run twice Layout #!/usr/bin/env bash set -Eeuo pipefail usage () { cat \u003c\u003c &#x27;EOF&#x27; Usage: rotate.sh [-d DIR] [-k DAYS] [-n] -d DIR directory to clean (required) -k DAYS keep files newer than DAYS (default 14) -n dry run, only print what would be removed EOF } main () { local dir = \"\" keep=14 dry=0 opt while getopts \":d:k:nh\" opt; do case $opt in d) dir = $OPTARG ;; k) keep= $OPTARG ;; n) dry=1 ;; h) usage; return 0 ;; :) echo \"option - $OPTARG needs a value\" >&2; return 64 ;; *) usage >&2; return 64 ;; esac done [[ -n $dir && -d $dir ]] || { echo \"error: -d must be an existing directory\" >&2; return 64; } [[ $keep =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] || { echo \"error: -k must be a number\" >&2; return 64; } if (( dry )); then find \" $dir \" - type f -mtime \"+ $keep \" - print else find \" $dir \" - type f -mtime \"+ $keep \" -delete fi } if [[ ${BASH_SOURCE[0]} == \" $0 \" ]]; then main \" $@ \" ; fi Putting logic in functions and calling main only when the file is run (not sourced) lets tests load the functions without running the tool. Exit codes and messages Use 0 for success, 1 for a general failure and 64 for a usage error (the convention from sysexits ). Send errors to standard error ( >&2 ) and normal output to standard output, so pipelines keep working. Make the message say what was wrong and what the user can do. Tests with bats #!/usr/bin/env bats setup() { TMP=$(mktemp -d); touch -d &#x27;30 days ago&#x27; \"$TMP/old.log\"; touch \"$TMP/new.log\"; } teardown() { rm -rf \"$TMP\"; } @test \"dry run lists old files and keeps them\" { run ./rotate.sh -d \"$TMP\" -k 14 -n [ \"$status\" -eq 0 ] [[ \"$output\" == *old.log* ]] [ -e \"$TMP/old.log\" ] } @test \"missing directory is a usage error\" { run ./rotate.sh -d /nope [ \"$status\" -eq 64 ] } Run shellcheck rotate.sh and bats tests/ in your pipeline. Use a temporary directory for test data and never point tests at real paths. Idempotence and safety A tool you may run twice should give the same result the second time. Prefer mkdir -p , ln -sfn , and checks such as \"does it exist\" before changes. Provide a dry-run option for anything destructive, and print what you are about to do. Predict first. A script is run with an option it does not know. What should it do? Your guess Answer: Print a usage message to standard error and exit with a non-zero code such as 64. Silent success hides mistakes. A clear message and a non-zero exit let callers and pipelines react. Incident: the cleanup that ran with the wrong variable Incident A cleanup script deletes the wrong directory because a path variable was empty after a typo in an option name. $ ./clean.sh --dri /data/tmp removing files under / The tool ignored unknown options and fell back to an empty directory value. The rewrite validated every option, required -d to be an existing directory, refused to run on / , offered a dry run, and had tests for each of these cases. Common mistakes No validation of required options Mixing messages and results on standard output Tests that touch real directories No dry-run mode for destructive tools"},"bash/temporary-files-and-atomic-writes":{"html":"\u003cp>A script writes a report straight into the file that a web page serves, and a visitor loads the page halfway through. Another script uses /tmp/work as a scratch file, and two runs overwrite each other. These are the everyday ways shell scripts corrupt data, and each has a small, standard cure.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Create unique temporary files and directories with mktemp\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Clean up with trap, even on failure\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Write a file atomically: write elsewhere, then rename\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Prevent overlapping runs with flock\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"never-invent-a-temporary-name\">Never invent a temporary name\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A fixed name such as \u003ccode>/tmp/work\u003c/code> is shared by every run, and another user can create it first and trick your script. \u003ccode>mktemp\u003c/code> creates a file or directory with a random name and safe permissions.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"tmp=$(mktemp)            # a new empty file, mode 600\nwork=$(mktemp -d)        # a new private directory\necho &quot;scratch&quot; &gt; &quot;$tmp&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">tmp=$(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">mktemp\u003c/span>)            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># a new empty file, mode 600\u003c/span>\nwork=$(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">mktemp\u003c/span> -d)        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># a new private directory\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;scratch&quot;\u003c/span> &gt; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$tmp\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Store the name in a variable and quote it every time you use it. Prefer a directory when a script needs several files, because one \u003ccode>rm -r\u003c/code> cleans them all.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"clean-up-with-trap\">Clean up with trap\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Temporary files must disappear even if the script fails or you press Ctrl+C. A \u003ccode>trap\u003c/code> on \u003ccode>EXIT\u003c/code> runs a command whenever the script ends, for any reason except a hard kill.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"#!/bin/bash\nset -euo pipefail\nwork=$(mktemp -d)\ntrap 'rm -rf &quot;$work&quot;' EXIT\n\ncurl -fsS https://example.com/data.json -o &quot;$work/data.json&quot;\njq .items &quot;$work/data.json&quot; &gt; &quot;$work/items.json&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-meta\">#!/bin/bash\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span> -euo pipefail\nwork=$(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">mktemp\u003c/span> -d)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">trap\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;rm -rf &quot;$work&quot;&#x27;\u003c/span> EXIT\n\ncurl -fsS https://example.com/data.json -o \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$work\u003c/span>/data.json&quot;\u003c/span>\njq .items \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$work\u003c/span>/data.json&quot;\u003c/span> &gt; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$work\u003c/span>/items.json&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Set the trap straight after creating the directory so that there is no gap in which a failure leaves it behind. Single quotes make the variable expand when the trap runs, not when it is set.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"write-atomically\">Write atomically\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Writing straight into the final file means readers can see it half finished, and a crash leaves a broken file. The pattern is: write a complete new file in the same directory, then rename it over the old one. A rename within one filesystem is atomic, so readers see either the old file or the new one, never a mix.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"out=/srv/www/report.html\nnew=$(mktemp &quot;$out.XXXXXX&quot;)        # same directory, same filesystem\ngenerate_report &gt; &quot;$new&quot;\nchmod 644 &quot;$new&quot;\nmv -f &quot;$new&quot; &quot;$out&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">out=/srv/www/report.html\nnew=$(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">mktemp\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$out\u003c/span>.XXXXXX&quot;\u003c/span>)        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># same directory, same filesystem\u003c/span>\ngenerate_report &gt; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$new\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">chmod\u003c/span> 644 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$new\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">mv\u003c/span> -f \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$new\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$out\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The temporary file must be in the same directory (or at least the same filesystem). Across filesystems \u003ccode>mv\u003c/code> copies and deletes, which is not atomic.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"one-copy-at-a-time-with-flock\">One copy at a time with flock\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>If a job runs every minute and sometimes takes two, you get two copies working on the same data. \u003ccode>flock\u003c/code> takes a lock on a file and releases it automatically when the process ends, even if it crashes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"#!/bin/bash\nexec 9&gt;/var/lock/report.lock\nif ! flock -n 9; then\n  echo &quot;report already running&quot; &gt;&amp;2\n  exit 0\nfi\n# ... the real work runs while the lock is held ...\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-meta\">#!/bin/bash\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exec\u003c/span> 9&gt;/var/lock/report.lock\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> ! flock -n 9; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">then\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;report already running&quot;\u003c/span> &gt;&amp;2\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exit\u003c/span> 0\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">fi\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># ... the real work runs while the lock is held ...\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The \u003ccode>-n\u003c/code> option fails at once instead of waiting. A hand-made lock file (\u003ccode>touch lock; ... rm lock\u003c/code>) is worse, because a crash leaves it behind and the job never runs again.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A script writes /etc/app.conf directly with `&gt;`, and the power fails halfway. What state may the file be in?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Truncated or half written, so the application may start with a broken configuration. Write a new file next to it and rename it over the old one.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Redirection with &gt; truncates the file first, then writes. A crash in between leaves partial content.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-half-a-report\">Incident: Half a report\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A nightly job regenerates the status page in place. At 02:00 customers sometimes saw a page that stopped in the middle of a table.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ls -l /srv/www/status.html\ngrep -n &quot;status.html&quot; /opt/jobs/status.sh\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> -l /srv/www/status.html\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">-rw-r--r-- 1 www www 18432 Jan 6 02:00 status.html   # normally 52 KB\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -n \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;status.html&quot;\u003c/span> /opt/jobs/status.sh\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">12: render_status &gt; /srv/www/status.html\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The script truncated the live file and then wrote it over several seconds. Visitors in that window got a partial page. The fix was to render into a temporary file in the same directory, check it was not empty, and rename it over the old file, and to add \u003ccode>flock\u003c/code> so that a slow run could not overlap the next one.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using fixed names in /tmp\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Creating the temporary file and the trap in the wrong order\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Renaming across filesystems and expecting it to be atomic\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using a plain lock file that a crash leaves behind\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"never-invent-a-temporary-name","text":"Never invent a temporary name"},{"id":"clean-up-with-trap","text":"Clean up with trap"},{"id":"write-atomically","text":"Write atomically"},{"id":"one-copy-at-a-time-with-flock","text":"One copy at a time with flock"},{"id":"incident-half-a-report","text":"Incident: Half a report"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"GNU Bash reference manual: traps","u":"https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/bash.html#index-trap"},{"t":"flock manual","u":"https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/flock.1.html"}],"text":"A script writes a report straight into the file that a web page serves, and a visitor loads the page halfway through. Another script uses /tmp/work as a scratch file, and two runs overwrite each other. These are the everyday ways shell scripts corrupt data, and each has a small, standard cure. What you will learn Create unique temporary files and directories with mktemp Clean up with trap, even on failure Write a file atomically: write elsewhere, then rename Prevent overlapping runs with flock Never invent a temporary name A fixed name such as /tmp/work is shared by every run, and another user can create it first and trick your script. mktemp creates a file or directory with a random name and safe permissions. tmp=$( mktemp ) # a new empty file, mode 600 work=$( mktemp -d) # a new private directory echo \"scratch\" > \" $tmp \" Store the name in a variable and quote it every time you use it. Prefer a directory when a script needs several files, because one rm -r cleans them all. Clean up with trap Temporary files must disappear even if the script fails or you press Ctrl+C. A trap on EXIT runs a command whenever the script ends, for any reason except a hard kill. #!/bin/bash set -euo pipefail work=$( mktemp -d) trap &#x27;rm -rf \"$work\"&#x27; EXIT curl -fsS https://example.com/data.json -o \" $work /data.json\" jq .items \" $work /data.json\" > \" $work /items.json\" Set the trap straight after creating the directory so that there is no gap in which a failure leaves it behind. Single quotes make the variable expand when the trap runs, not when it is set. Write atomically Writing straight into the final file means readers can see it half finished, and a crash leaves a broken file. The pattern is: write a complete new file in the same directory, then rename it over the old one. A rename within one filesystem is atomic, so readers see either the old file or the new one, never a mix. out=/srv/www/report.html new=$( mktemp \" $out .XXXXXX\" ) # same directory, same filesystem generate_report > \" $new \" chmod 644 \" $new \" mv -f \" $new \" \" $out \" The temporary file must be in the same directory (or at least the same filesystem). Across filesystems mv copies and deletes, which is not atomic. One copy at a time with flock If a job runs every minute and sometimes takes two, you get two copies working on the same data. flock takes a lock on a file and releases it automatically when the process ends, even if it crashes. #!/bin/bash exec 9>/var/lock/report.lock if ! flock -n 9; then echo \"report already running\" >&2 exit 0 fi # ... the real work runs while the lock is held ... The -n option fails at once instead of waiting. A hand-made lock file ( touch lock; ... rm lock ) is worse, because a crash leaves it behind and the job never runs again. Predict first. A script writes /etc/app.conf directly with `>`, and the power fails halfway. What state may the file be in? Your guess Answer: Truncated or half written, so the application may start with a broken configuration. Write a new file next to it and rename it over the old one. Redirection with > truncates the file first, then writes. A crash in between leaves partial content. Incident: Half a report Incident A nightly job regenerates the status page in place. At 02:00 customers sometimes saw a page that stopped in the middle of a table. $ ls -l /srv/www/status.html -rw-r--r-- 1 www www 18432 Jan 6 02:00 status.html # normally 52 KB $ grep -n \"status.html\" /opt/jobs/status.sh 12: render_status > /srv/www/status.html The script truncated the live file and then wrote it over several seconds. Visitors in that window got a partial page. The fix was to render into a temporary file in the same directory, check it was not empty, and rename it over the old file, and to add flock so that a slow run could not overlap the next one. Common mistakes Using fixed names in /tmp Creating the temporary file and the trap in the wrong order Renaming across filesystems and expecting it to be atomic Using a plain lock file t"},"bash/portable-scripts-and-shellcheck":{"html":"\u003cp>A script that worked on your laptop fails on the build server with &quot;bad substitution&quot;, and nobody can tell why. The server runs \u003ccode>sh\u003c/code>, which is not Bash. A little discipline about the shebang, the features you use, and an automatic linter prevents most of these surprises.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The difference between sh and bash and how to choose the shebang\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Common Bash-only features that break under sh\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using ShellCheck to find bugs before they run\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Readable style: functions, names, comments and usage text\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"sh-is-not-bash\">sh is not bash\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>#!/bin/sh\u003c/code> asks for a minimal POSIX shell. On Debian and Ubuntu that is \u003ccode>dash\u003c/code>, which is fast but lacks many Bash features. \u003ccode>#!/bin/bash\u003c/code> asks for Bash. Pick the one you really need, and write it on the first line.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"#!/usr/bin/env bash\nset -euo pipefail\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-meta\">#!/usr/bin/env bash\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span> -euo pipefail\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>env bash\u003c/code> finds Bash on the path, which helps on systems that keep it elsewhere. Features that only Bash has include arrays, \u003ccode>[[ ... ]]\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>${var,,}\u003c/code>, process substitution \u003ccode>&lt;(cmd)\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>pipefail\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>source\u003c/code>. If a script must run under plain \u003ccode>sh\u003c/code>, avoid all of them and test it with \u003ccode>dash script.sh\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"find-the-mistakes-with-shellcheck\">Find the mistakes with ShellCheck\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>ShellCheck reads a script and reports problems that are easy to miss: unquoted variables, wrong tests, useless uses of cat, and Bash-only features under an \u003ccode>sh\u003c/code> shebang. Each message has a code such as SC2086 that you can look up.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"shellcheck deploy.sh\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>shellcheck deploy.sh\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">In deploy.sh line 7:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">rm -rf $TARGET/*\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">       ^-- SC2086: Double quote to prevent globbing and word splitting.\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Run it in your editor and in the pipeline, and treat warnings as bugs. When you disagree, silence one line with a comment such as \u003ccode># shellcheck disable=SC2034\u003c/code> and explain why.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"readable-by-the-next-person\">Readable by the next person\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Scripts live longer than expected. A few habits make them easy to change at 03:00:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Rotate old reports. Usage: rotate.sh DIR DAYS\nset -euo pipefail\n\nusage() { echo &quot;usage: $0 DIR DAYS&quot; &gt;&amp;2; exit 2; }\n[[ $# -eq 2 ]] || usage\n\nreadonly dir=$1 days=$2\n\nmain() {\n  find &quot;$dir&quot; -type f -mtime +&quot;$days&quot; -print -delete\n}\nmain\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-meta\">#!/usr/bin/env bash\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># Rotate old reports. Usage: rotate.sh DIR DAYS\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span> -euo pipefail\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-function\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-title\">usage\u003c/span>\u003c/span>() { \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;usage: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$0\u003c/span> DIR DAYS&quot;\u003c/span> &gt;&amp;2; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">exit\u003c/span> 2; }\n[[ \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$#\u003c/span> -eq 2 ]] || usage\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">readonly\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">dir\u003c/span>=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$1\u003c/span> days=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$2\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-function\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-title\">main\u003c/span>\u003c/span>() {\n  find \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$dir\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> -\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">type\u003c/span> f -mtime +\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$days\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> -\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span> -delete\n}\nmain\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Put work in functions, name variables for what they hold, quote every expansion, and print a usage line for wrong input. Use \u003ccode>readonly\u003c/code> for values that must not change. Keep a short comment at the top that says what the script does and how to call it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"test-the-edges\">Test the edges\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Run the script on the situations that break it: an empty folder, a name with a space, no arguments, and a missing command. The \u003ccode>bats\u003c/code> test framework lets you write these checks as files, but even a few manual runs after \u003ccode>set -x\u003c/code> find most problems.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"bash -n script.sh        # syntax check only\nbash -x script.sh arg    # trace every command\nshellcheck -x script.sh  # follow sourced files\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>bash -n script.sh        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># syntax check only\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>bash -x script.sh arg    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># trace every command\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>shellcheck -x script.sh  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># follow sourced files\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A script starts with `#!/bin/sh` and uses `[[ -f $f ]]`. On Ubuntu it fails with &quot;[[: not found&quot;. Why?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> sh is dash there, and `[[` is a Bash feature. Change the shebang to bash, or rewrite the test with `[ -f &quot;$f&quot; ]`.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The shebang decides which shell runs the script, no matter which shell you typed the command in.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-works-on-my-laptop\">Incident: Works on my laptop\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A pipeline step that ran a deployment script failed only on the build machine with a syntax error on a line that looked fine.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"head -1 deploy.sh\nls -l /bin/sh\nsh deploy.sh\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -1 deploy.sh\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">#!/bin/sh\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> -l /bin/sh\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">lrwxrwxrwx 1 root root 4 /bin/sh -&gt; dash\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>sh deploy.sh\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">deploy.sh: 14: Syntax error: &quot;(&quot; unexpected\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The script used a Bash array. On the laptop, /bin/sh was Bash, so it worked; on the build server it is dash. The team changed the shebang to bash, added ShellCheck to the pipeline so this class of error fails the build, and documented that scripts use Bash 4 or newer.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using #!/bin/sh and then Bash-only features\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running scripts with sh script.sh and bypassing the shebang\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring ShellCheck warnings because the script works today\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving out usage text so nobody knows the arguments\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"sh-is-not-bash","text":"sh is not bash"},{"id":"find-the-mistakes-with-shellcheck","text":"Find the mistakes with ShellCheck"},{"id":"readable-by-the-next-person","text":"Readable by the next person"},{"id":"test-the-edges","text":"Test the edges"},{"id":"incident-works-on-my-laptop","text":"Incident: Works on my laptop"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"ShellCheck","u":"https://www.shellcheck.net/"},{"t":"POSIX shell command language","u":"https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9699919799/utilities/V3_chap02.html"}],"text":"A script that worked on your laptop fails on the build server with \"bad substitution\", and nobody can tell why. The server runs sh , which is not Bash. A little discipline about the shebang, the features you use, and an automatic linter prevents most of these surprises. What you will learn The difference between sh and bash and how to choose the shebang Common Bash-only features that break under sh Using ShellCheck to find bugs before they run Readable style: functions, names, comments and usage text sh is not bash #!/bin/sh asks for a minimal POSIX shell. On Debian and Ubuntu that is dash , which is fast but lacks many Bash features. #!/bin/bash asks for Bash. Pick the one you really need, and write it on the first line. #!/usr/bin/env bash set -euo pipefail env bash finds Bash on the path, which helps on systems that keep it elsewhere. Features that only Bash has include arrays, [[ ... ]] , ${var,,} , process substitution \u003c(cmd) , pipefail and source . If a script must run under plain sh , avoid all of them and test it with dash script.sh . Find the mistakes with ShellCheck ShellCheck reads a script and reports problems that are easy to miss: unquoted variables, wrong tests, useless uses of cat, and Bash-only features under an sh shebang. Each message has a code such as SC2086 that you can look up. $ shellcheck deploy.sh In deploy.sh line 7: rm -rf $TARGET/* ^-- SC2086: Double quote to prevent globbing and word splitting. Run it in your editor and in the pipeline, and treat warnings as bugs. When you disagree, silence one line with a comment such as # shellcheck disable=SC2034 and explain why. Readable by the next person Scripts live longer than expected. A few habits make them easy to change at 03:00: #!/usr/bin/env bash # Rotate old reports. Usage: rotate.sh DIR DAYS set -euo pipefail usage () { echo \"usage: $0 DIR DAYS\" >&2; exit 2; } [[ $# -eq 2 ]] || usage readonly dir = $1 days= $2 main () { find \" $dir \" - type f -mtime + \" $days \" - print -delete } main Put work in functions, name variables for what they hold, quote every expansion, and print a usage line for wrong input. Use readonly for values that must not change. Keep a short comment at the top that says what the script does and how to call it. Test the edges Run the script on the situations that break it: an empty folder, a name with a space, no arguments, and a missing command. The bats test framework lets you write these checks as files, but even a few manual runs after set -x find most problems. $ bash -n script.sh # syntax check only $ bash -x script.sh arg # trace every command $ shellcheck -x script.sh # follow sourced files Predict first. A script starts with `#!/bin/sh` and uses `[[ -f $f ]]`. On Ubuntu it fails with \"[[: not found\". Why? Your guess Answer: sh is dash there, and `[[` is a Bash feature. Change the shebang to bash, or rewrite the test with `[ -f \"$f\" ]`. The shebang decides which shell runs the script, no matter which shell you typed the command in. Incident: Works on my laptop Incident A pipeline step that ran a deployment script failed only on the build machine with a syntax error on a line that looked fine. $ head -1 deploy.sh #!/bin/sh $ ls -l /bin/sh lrwxrwxrwx 1 root root 4 /bin/sh -> dash $ sh deploy.sh deploy.sh: 14: Syntax error: \"(\" unexpected The script used a Bash array. On the laptop, /bin/sh was Bash, so it worked; on the build server it is dash. The team changed the shebang to bash, added ShellCheck to the pipeline so this class of error fails the build, and documented that scripts use Bash 4 or newer. Common mistakes Using #!/bin/sh and then Bash-only features Running scripts with sh script.sh and bypassing the shebang Ignoring ShellCheck warnings because the script works today Leaving out usage text so nobody knows the arguments"},"bash/debugging-shell-scripts":{"html":"\u003cp>A shell script that works on your laptop and fails at 03:00 in cron is one of the most common operations problems. The cause is rarely the logic. It is usually the environment, the quoting, or an error that nothing noticed. This lesson gives you tools that make a script show its work.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Tracing with \u003ccode>set -x\u003c/code> and a useful \u003ccode>PS4\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Strict mode and what it does not catch\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Finding mistakes before running with \u003ccode>shellcheck\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>bash -n\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Why scripts differ between a terminal and cron or CI\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"see-every-command-tracing\">See every command: tracing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"bash -x ./backup.sh                 # trace one run\nset -x                              # or turn tracing on inside the script\nexport PS4='+ ${BASH_SOURCE##*/}:${LINENO}: '   # show file and line for each traced command\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">bash -x ./backup.sh                 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># trace one run\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span> -x                              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># or turn tracing on inside the script\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">export\u003c/span> PS4=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;+ ${BASH_SOURCE##*/}:${LINENO}: &#x27;\u003c/span>   \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># show file and line for each traced command\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Each traced line starts with the \u003ccode>PS4\u003c/code> prefix, so you see which line ran and with which expanded values. Turn tracing on only around the suspect part (\u003ccode>set -x\u003c/code> ... \u003ccode>set +x\u003c/code>) to keep the output short, and remember it may print secrets.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"fail-loudly\">Fail loudly\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"#!/usr/bin/env bash\nset -Eeuo pipefail\ntrap 'echo &quot;failed at line $LINENO: $BASH_COMMAND&quot; &gt;&amp;2' ERR\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-meta\">#!/usr/bin/env bash\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span> -Eeuo pipefail\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">trap\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;echo &quot;failed at line $LINENO: $BASH_COMMAND&quot; &gt;&amp;2&#x27;\u003c/span> ERR\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>-e\u003c/code> exits on an error, \u003ccode>-u\u003c/code> treats unset variables as errors, \u003ccode>pipefail\u003c/code> makes a pipeline fail when any part fails, and the \u003ccode>ERR\u003c/code> trap tells you where. Strict mode is not magic: commands in an \u003ccode>if\u003c/code> test, in \u003ccode>&amp;&amp;\u003c/code> chains, or on the left of \u003ccode>||\u003c/code> are exempt from \u003ccode>-e\u003c/code>, so keep logic simple and check important results yourself.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"check-before-you-run\">Check before you run\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"bash -n backup.sh              # syntax check only, nothing runs\nshellcheck backup.sh\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>bash -n backup.sh              \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># syntax check only, nothing runs\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>shellcheck backup.sh\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">In backup.sh line 7:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">rm -rf $DIR/*\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">       ^--^ SC2086: Double quote to prevent globbing and word splitting.\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>shellcheck\u003c/code> finds quoting mistakes, unused variables and unsafe patterns. Run it in your pipeline so every change is checked.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"terminal-versus-cron\">Terminal versus cron\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Cron runs scripts with a minimal environment: a short \u003ccode>PATH\u003c/code>, no login files, a different working directory and no terminal.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Difference\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Symptom\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Fix\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>PATH\u003c/code> is short\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>command not found\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Use full paths or set \u003ccode>PATH\u003c/code> at the top\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Working directory differs\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Relative paths fail\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>cd &quot;$(dirname &quot;$0&quot;)&quot;\u003c/code> or use absolute paths\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>No profile loaded\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Missing variables\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Set variables in the script or an env file\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Output is mailed or lost\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>You never see errors\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Redirect to a log: \u003ccode>&gt;&gt; /var/log/backup.log 2&gt;&amp;1\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A script contains DIR=&quot;&quot; and then runs rm -rf $DIR/ . What does the command become?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> rm -rf / , which tries to delete everything it is allowed to.\u003c/p>\u003cp>An empty unquoted variable disappears. Always quote variables, use set -u so an unset variable stops the script, and check that the variable is not empty before a destructive command.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-works-by-hand-fails-at-night\">Incident: works by hand, fails at night\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A nightly backup script runs fine when started manually, but the cron job produces no archive and no error.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"crontab -l\nenv -i /bin/sh -c '/opt/scripts/backup.sh'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>crontab -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">0 2 * * * /opt/scripts/backup.sh\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">env\u003c/span> -i /bin/sh -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;/opt/scripts/backup.sh&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">/opt/scripts/backup.sh: line 9: aws: command not found\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Running the script with a clean environment (\u003ccode>env -i\u003c/code>) reproduced the failure: \u003ccode>aws\u003c/code> lived in \u003ccode>/usr/local/bin\u003c/code>, which cron&#39;s \u003ccode>PATH\u003c/code> does not include. The fix was a full path to the command, a \u003ccode>PATH\u003c/code> line at the top, and a log redirect, so the next failure leaves evidence.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Unquoted variables, which split on spaces and expand wildcards\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Assuming \u003ccode>set -e\u003c/code> catches every failure\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Debugging only in an interactive shell\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Sending cron output nowhere\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"see-every-command-tracing","text":"See every command: tracing"},{"id":"fail-loudly","text":"Fail loudly"},{"id":"check-before-you-run","text":"Check before you run"},{"id":"terminal-versus-cron","text":"Terminal versus cron"},{"id":"incident-works-by-hand-fails-at-night","text":"Incident: works by hand, fails at night"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"ShellCheck","u":"https://www.shellcheck.net/"},{"t":"GNU Bash manual: The Set Builtin","u":"https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/html_node/The-Set-Builtin.html"}],"text":"A shell script that works on your laptop and fails at 03:00 in cron is one of the most common operations problems. The cause is rarely the logic. It is usually the environment, the quoting, or an error that nothing noticed. This lesson gives you tools that make a script show its work. What you will learn Tracing with set -x and a useful PS4 Strict mode and what it does not catch Finding mistakes before running with shellcheck and bash -n Why scripts differ between a terminal and cron or CI See every command: tracing bash -x ./backup.sh # trace one run set -x # or turn tracing on inside the script export PS4= &#x27;+ ${BASH_SOURCE##*/}:${LINENO}: &#x27; # show file and line for each traced command Each traced line starts with the PS4 prefix, so you see which line ran and with which expanded values. Turn tracing on only around the suspect part ( set -x ... set +x ) to keep the output short, and remember it may print secrets. Fail loudly #!/usr/bin/env bash set -Eeuo pipefail trap &#x27;echo \"failed at line $LINENO: $BASH_COMMAND\" >&2&#x27; ERR -e exits on an error, -u treats unset variables as errors, pipefail makes a pipeline fail when any part fails, and the ERR trap tells you where. Strict mode is not magic: commands in an if test, in && chains, or on the left of || are exempt from -e , so keep logic simple and check important results yourself. Check before you run $ bash -n backup.sh # syntax check only, nothing runs $ shellcheck backup.sh In backup.sh line 7: rm -rf $DIR/* ^--^ SC2086: Double quote to prevent globbing and word splitting. shellcheck finds quoting mistakes, unused variables and unsafe patterns. Run it in your pipeline so every change is checked. Terminal versus cron Cron runs scripts with a minimal environment: a short PATH , no login files, a different working directory and no terminal. Difference Symptom Fix PATH is short command not found Use full paths or set PATH at the top Working directory differs Relative paths fail cd \"$(dirname \"$0\")\" or use absolute paths No profile loaded Missing variables Set variables in the script or an env file Output is mailed or lost You never see errors Redirect to a log: >> /var/log/backup.log 2>&1 Predict first. A script contains DIR=\"\" and then runs rm -rf $DIR/ . What does the command become? Your guess Answer: rm -rf / , which tries to delete everything it is allowed to. An empty unquoted variable disappears. Always quote variables, use set -u so an unset variable stops the script, and check that the variable is not empty before a destructive command. Incident: works by hand, fails at night Incident A nightly backup script runs fine when started manually, but the cron job produces no archive and no error. $ crontab -l 0 2 * * * /opt/scripts/backup.sh $ env -i /bin/sh -c &#x27;/opt/scripts/backup.sh&#x27; /opt/scripts/backup.sh: line 9: aws: command not found Running the script with a clean environment ( env -i ) reproduced the failure: aws lived in /usr/local/bin , which cron&#39;s PATH does not include. The fix was a full path to the command, a PATH line at the top, and a log redirect, so the next failure leaves evidence. Common mistakes Unquoted variables, which split on spaces and expand wildcards Assuming set -e catches every failure Debugging only in an interactive shell Sending cron output nowhere"}});